# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""BRYME Home & DIY desk — the Nigeria moat (Phase 1 T11, 21 Sep 2026).

Uncontested territory: harmattan, rainy season, borehole water, generator and
inverter maths, wiring red flags, mould after a flood — written for how homes
here actually behave. Tuple shape mirrors the other HOME_* modules:
(slug, kicker, title, dek, body-html). House rule: safety boundaries stated
without apology; no prices; anything that needs a licensed professional says so.
"""

HOME_LOCAL = [
 ("generator-vs-inverter-nigeria",
  "power",
  "Generator or inverter? The decision is a spreadsheet, not a brand",
  "Before you spend on either, run the arithmetic your salesperson skipped: your real watt-hours, your surge loads, and the one battery rule that decides whether the system survives year two.",
  """<p>Every power quote you receive is priced by kVA and Ah. What your house actually consumes is measured in watt-hours, and that arithmetic - five minutes with a notebook - decides whether your next power purchase feels like freedom or like a rented disappointment.</p>
<h2>Step one: write your real load</h2>
<p>For one honest week, list what you actually run during an outage and for how long: fridge (compressor cycles roughly a third of the hour, not full-time), fans, TV and decoder, router, lights, laptops, water pump. Nameplate watts are on each appliance's sticker; multiply by hours and add up - that's your watt-hour demand per outage. Most homes discover their "big generator instinct" is three times the actual need, and their "small inverter shortcut" is half the real one.</p>
<h2>Step two: surges, not averages</h2>
<p>Motors - fridge compressor, pumping jack, freezer, any borehole pump - pull two to three times their running watts for a few seconds on start. An inverter or generator sized to the sum of running watts will brown out on the evening the pump and fridge decide to wake up together. Sum the running watts, add the largest single motor's surge headroom, and buy against that number. This is the spec salespeople round down.</p>
<h2>Step three: the battery rule</h2>
<p>If you go inverter: usable capacity is roughly half your nominal kWh for lead-acid chemistry (deep cycling a car battery daily kills it in months - the "tubular" upgrade buys time, not immunity), and lithium tolerates much deeper cycles but still ages by heat. Nigerian roof-space heat is the silent budget-killer: a battery in a hot cupboard in year one is a replacement quote in year three. Ventilate the battery room, keep it off the floor, and ask for the installation's battery temperature story before you ask about warranty paper.</p>
<h2>Where each one genuinely wins</h2>
<ul><li><b>Generator:</b> cheap energy per hour while you burn fuel, tireless through long outages, tolerant of a full house running at once. Its true costs are fuel price volatility, service intervals you must actually keep, and noise your neighbours are pricing into their own patience.</li>
<li><b>Inverter + batteries:</b> silent, instant, phone-charging-friendly, and the only option that becomes <em>cheaper</em> over time once solar panels join it. Its true cost is capacity bought upfront: you cannot "borrow" extra stored sunlight on a harmattan week.</li>
<li><b>The hybrid answer almost everyone wants:</b> a small generator wired to charge a real battery bank (changeover done by a licensed electrician with a proper changeover switch and earthing - the death-avoidance part of the diagram), so the generator runs at its efficient steady load to fill batteries instead of babysitting every bulb.</li></ul>
<h2>The check before you buy anything</h2>
<p>Whatever the quote says, make it write: running watts covered, surge watts covered, battery chemistry and usable depth, and - if solar-ready - whether the inverter accepts panels today or needs a replacement later. A vendor who can answer those four questions in writing is selling a system. One who answers "trust me, it'll carry your house" is selling tomorrow's phone call.</p>"""),

 ("harmattan-and-your-electronics",
  "seasonal",
  "Harmattan is hard on screens, laptops and printers - the five-minute fixes that work",
  "Dry air and suspended dust are a chemistry set your devices were not shipped with. Nothing here needs a technician: it needs a calendar and a microfibre cloth.",
  """<p>For six to ten weeks a year, the air in your house is carrying fine mineral dust and holding almost no humidity, and your electronics pay for it in three currencies: heat, static and jammed moving parts. Every fix below is prevention; the cure, once dust has cooked onto a fan blade or paper has curled in a tray, is service fees.</p>
<h2>What actually happens to your devices</h2>
<ul><li><b>Fan intakes cake first.</b> Laptops on beds and sofas - where the intakes are blocked <em>and</em> the fabric is shedding - build a felt layer on the fan within one season. That layer is insulation with a motor attached.</li>
<li><b>Static stops chips slowly.</b> A dry room with synthetic carpet and a nylon chair is a charge generator. Sudden laptop death with no warning is usually not the dry air itself - it's the discharge that happened when someone reached across the desk to touch the open chassis.</li>
<li><b>Screens abrade.</b> Dust on a panel isn't dirt, it's grit; wiping a dry screen with a tissue is sandpaper with confidence.</li>
<li><b>Printers jam on paper mood.</b> Low humidity makes sheet edges curl and static-hold to each other; the top sheet of an old ream is where all the feed failures start.</li></ul>
<h2>The season kit (ten minutes a week)</h2>
<p>One: laptops onto hard trays or lap-desks, off textiles, intakes facing air they can steal - a cheap hard tray works. Two: open the printer cover, fan the stacked paper before loading, and keep the ream in its wrapper with a folded flap when idle. Three: wipe screens <em>after</em> blowing loose dust off (a camera blower or the keyboard trick: short bursts from the far edge), cloth barely damp, never sprayed on the panel. Four: before touching any open chassis - desktop case, TV back, console - touch a grounded metal part of it first, and keep the humidifier on the desk, not the floor. Five: once, mid-season, take the laptop to a shaded table and blow the vents out from the exhaust side while it runs off charge; a year's felt, in ten seconds.</p>
<h2>The humidifier trap nobody warns you about</h2>
<ul><li>Ultrasonic misters scatter whatever is in their water - including salt - onto everything within reach as a fine conductive film. Near electronics, use distilled or boiled-and-cooled water without exception, and point the mist away from desks. A "humidified" desk under a hard-water mister is a corrosion experiment you're funding.</li></ul>
<h2>When to call the professional</h2>
<p>Anything that involves opening a sealed power supply, a TV that clicks and won't light, or a laptop that runs hot <em>after</em> a dust clear - that's the desk's boundary, stated without apology: capacitor charge and glued panels are where YouTube lessons end and burnt mains begin. Fans, filters, paper and cloth are entirely yours.</p>"""),

 ("rainy-season-home-checklist",
  "seasonal",
  "The pre-rains home checklist that Lagos roofs actually need",
  "Gutters, sealant, ceiling pencil-marks, drainage fall and the weep holes nobody remembers: a Saturday of small inspections that turns a leaking ceiling into a paragraph in this checklist instead.",
  """<p>Water does not attack a house all at once; it queues. Every rainy-season emergency on a Nigerian roof has a dry-season version - a half-blocked gutter, a cracked seal, a downpipe pointed at the wall - and each one costs an hour before the rains to fix and a month of repainting after. This is that hour, ordered so the ladder work happens once.</p>
<h2>Roof and gutters (dry boots, two people, morning light)</h2>
<ul><li><b>The bucket test.</b> Clear every gutter and downpipe by hand, then pour two full buckets into the far end of each run. It should leave the downpipe at the ground in seconds, in a steady stream. Anything that backs up, drips at a joint or exits slower than the next bucket proves is a blockage or a fall problem - fix it before the first storm decides for you.</li>
<li><b>Sealant and screws.</b> Walk the parapet and flashings: sheet-to-wall junctions, vent pipes, the screw lines on every roof sheet. Sealant that crumbles to powder under a fingernail is gone, whatever the colour says. Re-bed it; missing screws get replaced with the same neoprene-washer type, not a smaller one.</li>
<li><b>The pencil habit.</b> Mark the corners of every ceiling stain with a dated pencil note on the floorboard behind furniture, not the wall. After the first heavy rain, a stain that grew or a new mark is an active leak with a postcode; a stain that sat unchanged for a season is history. This one pencil is the difference between "repaint" and "find the sheet first."</li></ul>
<h2>Windows and walls</h2>
<p>Slide-frame aluminium windows have weep holes in the outer track - small slots meant to let wind-blown rain out. Construction cement and years of neglect seal them shut, and then the frame becomes a fish tank for your wall. Clear every one with a cocktail stick and a damp rag before the rains. Outside: confirm the ground <em>falls away</em> from the foundation on all sides; a flat apron that puddles against the wall is rising damp on a payment plan. Check the paint at skirting height - chalky, bubbling or crumbling there means the ground, not the roof.</p>
<h2>Power and water, the parts people forget</h2>
<ul><li>Generator and inverter: outdoor units get a raised, drained, roofed position <em>and</em> an exhaust that points away from every window - the rain-storm habit of closing windows around a running generator is how carbon monoxide gets invited indoors; keep the intake side of the house clear and the machine outside, always. (This is the desk's hard line, not a suggestion.)</li>
<li>Water tanks: covers sealed gap-free - storm water pooling on top of an open tank is a mosquito nursery with a maintenance schedule; flush the sediment from the bottom tap before the rains dilute what you're storing.</li></ul>
<h2>The ten-minute wrap</h2>
<p>Photograph the cleared gutters, the pencil marks, the fresh sealant lines, and the dated note goes in the house file. Not for anyone else: when a leak appears next year, your photo set tells a roofer where to look first, and that sentence is worth more than the gutter itself.</p>"""),

 ("borehole-water-and-your-kettle",
  "water",
  "Borehole water, kettle scale and your filter: what actually needs solving",
  "Why white flakes are chemistry, not poison; which problems a boil fixes and which it makes worse; and the filter order that makes sense for a Nigerian borehole.",
  """<p>Nigerian borehole water arrives with three completely different problems that everyone solves as if they were one: hardness, sediment and the occasional biological surprise. A kettle tells you about the first, a white towel about the second, and - for the third - nothing visible does anything at all, which is why the boring test matters.</p>
<h2>Scale is not dirt</h2>
<p>White flakes in a boiled kettle, grey film on a stainless sink, laundry that needs more soap than the packet swears: that's dissolved calcium and magnesium - temporary hardness - precipitating when heated. It is a plumbing-and-appliance issue, not a health one, and boiling <em>concentrates</em> the visible part of it into your kettle element instead of removing it from your glass. What you do about it is engineering, not anxiety: a water softener (salt-dosed, regenerates, needs a drain point) for whole-house appliance protection, or nothing but descaling ritual for the kettle if scale is your only complaint. Magnetic "conditioners" are a cheap science-fair toy at best; don't fund one.</p>
<h2>Sediment, and the order filters go in</h2>
<p>If the towel test turns brown after a minute of cold tap water - fine silt, laterite cloud after rain, or a silty run after the pump kicks - you have suspended solids, and the solution is not expensive, it is ordered: <b>sediment first, carbon second, anything finer third.</b> A spun polypropylene sock (changed when flow visibly drops, not by calendar) eats the silt that would choke every downstream cartridge and scratch every tap valve. Carbon after it improves taste, chlorine where any exists, and the organics that make water "off" without making it dangerous. Skipping to an RO unit as your first move is how homes buy a machine that its own prefilter would have protected - and pay for the waste water RO rejects to fix a problem a cheap sediment sock solved.</p>
<h2>The test no filter can smell</h2>
<p>Boiling is a treatment, not a verdict: it kills germs if given a proper rolling minute, but it destroys nothing - it doesn't remove nitrate, salt, metals or the agricultural cocktail that shallow wells near soak-aways can carry. So once a year, for a small lab fee at a university or state water lab, hand them a bottle of your water <em>after</em> your filters and ask for the two-line answer that matters at home: bacterial contamination indicators, and the dissolved-solids/salt figure. That sheet, kept with the house file, is what "our water is fine" should mean - instead of an opinion about clarity.</p>
<h2>And the tank, always</h2>
<p>Everything above assumes the storage tank is honest: lid sealed against birds and leaves, vent meshed, no sunlit wall for algae, and drained-scrushed-flushed on a rhythm you actually keep (most homes: once a year, before the rains refill everything you stored). The treatment plant is the tank, first - every filter you buy is really insurance you didn't want to need.</p>"""),

 ("wiring-red-flags-in-your-home",
  "safety",
  "Eight wiring signs in a finished Nigerian home that mean call a professional",
  "Warm sockets, dimming lights, daisy-changed breakers, the missing earth - the visible symptoms of problems that become fires at 2 a.m., and the exact sentence to say to an electrician.",
  """<p>Most houses in this country carry wiring installed by the seat of someone's pants - competent in their habit, not in your standards. The reassuring part of electrical faults is that almost every serious one announces itself visibly and early. This page is a translation guide for those announcements, plus the boundary: what you may tighten, what you must hire.</p>
<h2>Call-a-professional signs, ranked by how fast they matter</h2>
<ul><li><b>A socket or plug that is warm, smells of hot plastic, or has brown ghost-marks around the pins.</b> That's resistance heating at a loose connection - a fire that has begun its paperwork. Stop using the socket today, and "today" is the entire warning.</li>
<li><b>Lights dim or bulbs surge when a large appliance starts.</b> Classic loose neutral or overloaded joint - the supply is being shared through a connection smaller than it should be. This one is not cosmetic and not "old houses do that."</li>
<li><b>No earth wire at three-pin sockets.</b> A strip of green-yellow is not decoration; without it an appliance fault makes the metal case - the fridge, the water heater - the live conductor through whoever touches it. Any socket cover you open to find bare copper ends and no earth is a rewiring conversation, not a repair.</li>
<li><b>Twisted tape joins inside walls or above ceilings.</b> Where a junction box should be, a "handled with tape and confidence" splice waits. Tape dries; joints oxidise; faults don't. Every hidden join should sit in an accessible box with a proper connector.</li>
<li><b>One breaker that won't stay in, or trips when the kettle <em>and</em> anything else run.</b> Either the circuit is overloaded past its cable's rating - breakers protect cables, so a breaker that's fighting its job means the cable is losing it - or the breaker itself is tired. Neither is a swap-shop job; both need an actual load check.</li>
<li><b>A board where circuits are shared by improvisation:</b> kitchen on the lights' breaker, AC on a "twin" slot crammed into a space for one device, the spare ways stacked double. Every legitimate panel spare that got two breakers where one sat is how this list begins.</li>
<li><b>Burnt, blackened or "melted-sweet" plastic at the meter tails or changeover.</b> Supply side: hands off, notify the service provider and get a licensed contractor with their paperwork for anything ahead of your main switch.</li>
<li><b>An RCD that has never been tested or never trips on test.</b> The little test button exists for one ritual: press it every quarter. No trip from a live safety device is no safety device.</li></ul>
<h2>What you may actually fix yourself</h2>
<p>With the breaker off and a confirmed dead with your own tester: re-clip a faceplate, replace a switch on the same terminals, swap a flex on a lamp. Anything that runs <em>inside</em> a wall, spans a joint, touches the board, or involves the meter - professional. This desk states its boundary without apology: water, aluminium mains and "small corrections at night" are the three words nearest every tragedy in the news.</p>
<h2>The sentence to say when you hire</h2>
<p>"Test first, quote second, certificate with the work." A real electrician can explain load and circuit for every breaker in your board, will never laugh at the warm-socket question, and expects you to ask for what the wiring in your house is actually made of. If the conversation starts with "how much for me to just add a socket here," the correct answer is a different electrician.</p>"""),

 ("mould-after-a-flooded-room",
  "safety",
  "Mould after a flood or a leaking roof: the 48-hour window and the cut line",
  "What can be saved, what belongs in a bin bag, why bleach on drywall is theatre, and the two moves in the first two days that decide the rest of the room's life.",
  """<p>Mould after a flood is not a cleaning job; it's a race - the first forty-eight hours belong to you, after that the wall chooses. Everything on this page is boring on purpose, because the proven wins here are dehumidifiers, plastic sheeting and judgement calls about what is porous.</p>
<h2>The two moves that come before scrubbing</h2>
<p>One: stop the source. A "cleaned" ceiling above an unsealed roof sheet is a cleaning subscription. Two: get the room <em>dry with force</em> - cross-draft fans, any dehumidifier you can borrow, nothing left damp for the weather to finish the job the flood started. Within 48 hours of wetting, most materials are recoverable; beyond it, porous ones are already colonised. Rush the drying; delay the cosmetics.</p>
<h2>Keep, bin, or cut: the honest rule</h2>
<ul><li><b>Bin list, no debate:</b> anything porous that was under floodwater - mattresses, upholstered furniture that absorbed it, carpet underlay, compressed-board furniture that swelled, any ceiling tile. Floodwater is not rain; what it carried into foam is why the bin is the answer. Seal each item in its own bag on the way out of the room - mould dry-scraped off a mattress is the spore cloud that becomes your lungs.</li>
<li><b>Keep list:</b> solid timber (scrub, then dry slowly out of sun), metal, glass, hard plastics, and concrete block - all cleanable once the water they met was clean-ish. Salvageable with work: rugs and clothing that met <em>rain</em> water, washed hot within the day.</li>
<li><b>The cut line for drywall/plasterboard:</b> board that wicked water above 300 mm from the floor, or that sounds hollow and soft, doesn't dry "back to new" - it dries as a sandwich with a damp core. The professional rule the pros actually follow: cut out affected board <em>up to a line about 300 mm above the highest tide mark</em>, bag from the room, and leave the cavity open and blowing air until everything behind your cut is measurably dry. Patching paint over wet board is how a clean-up becomes a re-build.</li></ul>
<h2>Bleach is theatre on the wrong surface</h2>
<p>On tile, glass and sealed metal: dilute household bleach, scrub, rinse, dry - fine. On plasterboard, wood or grout behind furniture, bleach mostly delivers water to a porous surface while the smell theatrically announces "done" - roots of mould below paint survive spraying. What actually works on surfaces is: HEPA-vacuum dry growth first, scrub with detergent solution, dry thoroughly, then and only then decide whether paint is the last step of dryness, not the first lie. Wear an N95 and gloves for all of it; seal the room off from the house with a taped plastic sheet in the doorway, and if the affected area is bigger than roughly a square metre of wall, that's the size where professional remediation pays for itself in not breathing your own repair.</p>
<h2>The week after: keep it from returning</h2>
<p>Furniture off walls, wardrobes angled to breathe (the corner of a built-in against an external wall is this country's favourite mould colony), bathroom door open after every bath, kitchen extraction while cooking, and the humidity gauge you can buy cheaply learning to read 60 percent in a tropical ground floor as "act now, not smell later." A flooded room that stays mould-free is won with airflow for the rest of its life, not with the one bottle of anti-mould wash. This desk's boundary, stated plainly: structural cracks, sewage water at any scale, and any room where your family includes an asthma attack or a compromised immune system - those start at the professional, not the checklist.</p>"""),

 ("circuit-breaker-tripped-not-mystery",
  "FIX",
  "The breaker keeps tripping and no one is touching anything",
  "Breakers do not trip out of malice. Five causes in order of how fast you can test them, and the one that means stop touching the panel.",
  """<p>A breaker that trips once is an event. A breaker that trips daily is a message - and the message is almost never "the breaker is weak," which is what most advice on the street concludes first and fastest, usually because it is the cheapest thing to replace. Work through the causes in test order before buying anything.</p>
<h2>Test five minutes first</h2>
<ul><li><b>One circuit, one appliance.</b> Reset, then use nothing on that circuit. If it holds, add appliances one at a time until it trips: you have just found the fault, and it is the appliance - usually a heating element in an iron, kettle or water heater whose insulation has started leaking to the chassis.</li>
<li><b>The trip timing tells the story.</b> Trips the instant you switch on = a short or a dead element. Trips after minutes of running = overload or heat creeping into a loose connection. Trips at random, nothing running = the panel or the wiring itself, and the next two bullets are why you call someone.</li>
<li><b>Sniff the panel safely.</b> Open it only by daylight, touch nothing: a warm or fishy smell from breakers or busbar, discoloured plastic, an arc scar on a breaker window - any of these is a loose-connection burn starting, which is the only thing on this list that means stop and call before you read the rest of this page.</li>
<li><b>RCD/GFCI outlets or a breaker with a test button?</b> Press the test button: if it does not click open, your earth-leakage protection is decorative - a separate problem, and an important one in a house where the earth rod may have rusted out of the loop years ago.</li>
<li><b>Two breakers, one appliance, same trip.</b> The appliance is fine and the circuit is shared or mislabelled - someone extended a ring and never told the panel. Map it honestly and move the load.</li></ul>
<h2>What not to accept</h2>
<p>The fix that is "a bigger breaker": upsizing the breaker to stop the trips when the wire is the undersized thing is how houses burn politely, months later inside the wall. The wire gauge is the limit, the breaker is the referee - a referee who refuses to send anyone off is not the solution. If the electrician's whole answer is "change the breaker" and the panel is otherwise quiet, get the second opinion this article was researched to justify.</p>
<h2>What this desk cannot see for you</h2>
<p>Which of the five is yours is a diagnosis of your wiring, your appliances and your panel, made by a competent person with a meter and, for anything inside the wall, a licence. This page is the language to arrive with - and the test list that means you pay for a repair, not a guess.</p>"""),

 ("harmattan-fire-safety-house",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "Harmattan is also a fire season: eleven checks before the dry nights peak",
  "Dry air turns ordinary faults into flame faster than any other month does. The walk-through that takes twenty minutes and the habits that cost nothing.",
  """<p>Between December and February the humidity collapses, everything in the house becomes tinder-grade, and the same faults that shrug off risk in the wet months stop shrugging. This is the harmattan fire walk-through - not fear, inventory.</p>
<h2>Twenty minutes, top to bottom</h2>
<ul><li><b>Charging things off the floor.</b> Phone and torch chargers on a mattress, a curtain's reach, or a plastic surface: move them to a hard, clear spot - and never leave a battery charging on a bed while the room is empty, whatever the month.</li>
<li><b>The heater's one-metre rule.</b> Anything electric that glows or blows heat gets one metre of empty air on every side, away from the curtain that has started pooling near it, and off the rug. The dryer rack is not a heater.</li>
<li><b>Extension bars and their daisy-chain.</b> One heater on one bar, on its own, and check the bar's rating while you're there - a chain of bars feeding a heating load through a thin cable is how harmattan house-fires start in a room that felt tidy.</li>
<li><b>Cooking with windows open, the burn risk of.</b> Harmattan wind plus a small flame behind a window someone closed means a draught moving a curtain; tie the curtain, move the curtain, or cook with the window the other room opens to.</li>
<li><b>Candles and torches - the season's own habit.</b> Outages mean light that has a flame in it. Every candle needs a metal or ceramic base on a shelf nothing above can fall onto; a candle in a jar of water on a windowsill is free and wind-proof. The rechargeable torch with a fresh bulb is the whole solution, so make sure it has one.</li>
<li><b>The ceiling fan's dust.</b> Thick dust on fan blades is fuel with a motor attached - in the dry months it can burn just from heat and friction; wipe the blades and listen for the smell of a warming motor while you're on the ladder.</li>
<li><b>Stored dry goods and paper.</b> Cardboard corners, newsprint for wrapping, the kerosene lamp fuel (if you keep one) - all of it moves away from the electrical panel, the heater's corner and the kitchen stove line. Kerosene lives outside the kitchen, sealed, in a container with a lid.</li>
<li><b>Smoke alarm, if you own one: test it. If you don't:</b> a battery alarm in the corridor outside the bedrooms is the cheapest protective device any house can hold. Put it on the wall tonight, not next month.</li>
<li><b>Exit rehearsal, two minutes:</b> where does everyone come out, where is the key to the gate at night (locked courtyard doors turn a small fire into a big one - keep a torch and the key on the same hook), and who calls the responder; learn the number of the nearest station with the response vehicle, not the one with the best map position.</li>
<li><b>Bikes, gens and fuel:</b> no generator in an enclosed space even in the cold - the same carbon monoxide that no smell warns you about is deadlier in a closed harmattan house - and no fuelling a hot engine; the residual heat finds the vapour.</li>
<li><b>Roof-space lamp and the solar-rig charge controller:</b> dusty connections and a controller cooking in the sun room; check both by daylight, hands off the bare terminals, eyes on the colour of the plastic.</li></ul>
<h2>The habit list (free, daily)</h2>
<p>Switches off at the wall at night - the standby light on an appliance is current that never gets a holiday; heaters are off when the room is empty, chargers when people sleep. And the one sentence to repeat in a house with a courtyard gate: <em>the way out must open faster than the fire grows.</em> No page replaces a working alarm, a rehearsed exit, and a fire you have not started.</p>"""),

 ("ceiling-water-stain-removal",
  "FIX",
  "The brown stain on your ceiling is a map: read it before you paint over it",
  "Active leak versus old scar, how to test in five minutes with kitchen paper, and the stain sequence that actually stops the ghost coming back.",
  """<p>A ceiling stain is a record of water, and like any record it has a date. The date decides whether you are cleaning or investigating - and the worst habit in Nigerian home maintenance is the roller of white paint across a stain that was trying to tell you something.</p>
<h2>Active or archive? Five minutes with kitchen paper</h2>
<p>Press a folded paper towel flat on the darkest part of the stain for a minute, then feel it: damp or warped = water is present now, and the conversation is with the roof or the bathroom above, not the paint aisle. Dry, but the stain has a ring with a darker edge like a tree's ring - that's a tide mark, meaning it is <em>still moving</em>, seasonally, slowly - investigate too. Bone dry with a soft crusty bloom = an old scar; safe to clean and seal. If the ceiling is soft anywhere - press a knuckle gently - skip everything below and go to the leak itself; a spongy board is already part of the structure's future decision.</p>
<h2>Fix the water, then the cosmetic</h2>
<p>The source list for upstairs-or-over-ceiling water: the bathroom-above floor junctions and the grout lines around the shower wall, the trap under any sink on that floor, a toilet seal that fails only on flush (watch the stain for the hour after use - that is the whole diagnostic), and for the top floor: slab cracks, the parapet edge where the waterproofing has gone chalky, and plumbing vents. The pencil-test trick from the rain season - mark the stain's edge - works here too: an edge advancing without rain means an indoor source.</p>
<h2>The stain-removal sequence (after the leak is dead certain)</h2>
<ul><li><b>Wash the halo:</b> warm water with a little detergent, sponge, two passes - ceiling paint is soft and scrubbing it to a shine is its own failure; the water ring lives in the surface, so get the ring, not the centre first.</li>
<li><b>Kill what's left under the paint:</b> water stains bleed through ordinary emulsion, because the stain is tannin - the same chemistry as tea - and emulsion is water-based. A shellac-type or stain-blocking primer, painted only on the stain's area like a spot-weld, is the product; every shop sells one under some brand - ask for exactly "stain blocker," not "sealer."</li>
<li><b>Paint the panel, not the spot:</b> once primed, roll the ceiling's own paint across the whole panel between its corners - a spot-painted ceiling shows its repair under raking light forever, and the raking light is the window you forgot.</li>
<li><b>Black specks with the stain?</b> That is soot from a kerosene lamp or candle below, not water; a degreasing wash handles it and no primer drama is needed. And a stain that smells musty three days after the rain stopped needs the anti-mould wipe in the leak article's playbook before it becomes the next one.</li></ul>
<h2>One-line house rule</h2>
<p>Any stain you seal must be a stain you have tested. Paint over the active one and you have bought yourself eighteen months of watching it reappear in the same shape, bigger - and an estimate next year for the ceiling that sagged while you weren't looking.</p>"""),

 ("compound-mosquito-control-night",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "Mosquito control that works on a compound: the four-hour rule beats the fogger",
  "Why spraying the air is theatre, the standing-water audit nobody does, and the screen-and-fan strategy that cuts the night bite count properly.",
  """<p>The evening mosquito on a Nigerian compound is not an air problem to spray away - it is a breeding problem with an address, and the females that bite you were made within fifty metres of where you sit. Foggers kill some of the flying generation tonight and zero of next week's. Here is the audit that actually lowers the bite count, ordered by payoff.</p>
<h2>The standing-water audit (the whole ballgame)</h2>
<ul><li><b>Walk the compound after dark with a torch:</b> every vessel that can hold water an adult female can raise children in - blocked gutter and its channel against the wall, tyres, the saucers under pots (the most beautiful garden in the city is a hatchery), buckets left outside, the water storage without a lid clamped down, a discarded carton in the corner the wind blew behind the fence. Tip, scrub, cover, drain - the larvae need calm water and the eggs need the vessel to stay damp between rains, so <em>scrubbing</em> the inside matters as much as pouring.</li>
<li><b>The one-a-week rhythm:</b> mosquitoes are faster than seasons - seven days is roughly the water-to-biter schedule, so the audit lives or dies on its calendar, not its thoroughness.</li>
<li><b>Ornaments and the obvious:</b> the birdbath gets scrubbed, the vase water changes weekly, the AC's drip tray is the quietest breeding tank in the city's homes - check it, and clear its drain where it runs into the wall.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Barriers that do the real night work</h2>
<p>Sleep under a treated net, because the dawn and dusk feeders do their damage when you are unconscious and no spray survives a whole night of fans. Window screens on the bedrooms - mesh costs money once and screens keep the house breathable with the lights on, which is the difference between a compound you can sit in and a house you retreat inside. The door you leave open for the evening breeze is the road in: the fan in a doorway blows harder than any insect's flight speed and costs nothing but a socket - the airflow barrier is the one trick renters and owners can both do tonight.</p>
<h2>The things that are theatre</h2>
<ul><li><b>Coils and plug-in liquid vaporisers</b> push the numbers down in a closed room and lose most of it the moment a window is open - use them in the bedroom before you enter, with the door shut, not as an invisible force field for the sitting area. <b>Herb-burn smokes</b> repel by making the room unpleasant for everything including you, and the larvae stay happy downstairs. <b>Ultrasound apps and wrist bands</b>: no measurable benefit at any honest dose of hope.</li>
<li><b>The estate fogger:</b> useful for the immediate swarm at the gate - it's an insecticide event, not control - and it will not reach what matters, the water no one emptied.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When to escalate</h2>
<p>A sustained bite load after all of the above means a source bigger than your compound: a neighbour's blocked plot, the vacant lot with its tyres, a shared tank with its lip - that is a block-level conversation and the vector-control people exist for the public end of it. On the personal side: a fever in the season with the bite count means the clinic and the test, not this page - this desk handles the mosquitoes; the illness is the doctor's desk.</p>
<h2>Two lines to remember</h2>
<p>Empty what holds water once a week; blow a fan across the places you cannot screen. Everything else is marketing with a nozzle - and the bite count will tell you which of these two was the one you needed.</p>"""),

 ("gentle-low-flow-fixes",
  "FIX",
  "The tap runs thin and nobody will blame the plumber: five gentle fixes",
  "The aerator that ruins everything, the sediment sock, the valve that never fully opened after the tank work - low flow is usually three minutes of your own time, not a pump.",
  """<p>A tap that sighs instead of running is one of the most misdiagnosed faults in a house with stored water - everyone reaches for the pump, the "pressure problem," the plumber's invoice. Most thin flow is mineral and grit, caught deliberately by the fixtures the engineers designed to catch it - and those traps are meant to be cleaned, not worshipped.</p>
<h2>Three minutes, in order</h2>
<ul><li><b>The aerator.</b> Every modern tap has a threaded mesh screen in its nose to mix air into the stream and meet water-efficiency rules - which also makes it a perfect sediment sieve. Unscrew it (a coin edge or fingers; cloth if it's chromed), soak the little insert in vinegar for twenty minutes, brush its mesh soft, refit. This single job fixes more "low-pressure taps" than any plumber in the city will admit to doing.</li>
<li><b>The shut-offs that were never reopened.</b> After any tank work, a tap under the sink, or the isolated fixture's own valve, someone turned and half-turned: check the tap's isolation valves and the fixture's inlet connections - and the storage tank's own outlet valve, which the handyman closes "while at it" and then never remembers. A main that is a quarter turn from open makes the whole house run thirsty - the same symptom as a failing pump, at the cost of a wrist.</li>
<li><b>The single-lever mixer's cartridge.</b> A mixer only hot or only cold or stubborn in the middle usually has debris in the cartridge - isolate both lines, undo the handle, pull the cartridge, flush the seat, check the little rubber seals and the flow-restrictor washer in the inlet for grit - then refit, and if it still runs the same, the same cartridge is a shelf item at any serious plumbing counter for less than a plumber's call-out.</li>
<li><b>Kitchen and bathroom taps are different animals:</b> the pull-out kitchen spray head has its own clog-prone face plate - soak its face in vinegar in a bag tied around the head for an hour; the showerhead with its rubber nubs is meant to be rubbed clean with a thumb while running - every nub that clears is a millilitre you got back.</li>
<li><b>Grit from the tank itself.</b> If the aerators keep clogging weekly, the problem is upstream - the sediment sock from the well article (change it; grey, not white, means it is doing its job), or the tank needs its own cleaning day - and that is a whole separate page on this desk.</li></ul>
<h2>When it is genuinely pressure</h2>
<p>Thin flow at <em>every</em> fixture at once, the tank full, the valves wide open, the aerators clean - then and only then is it a pump, a header height or a partially collapsed supply line. The order matters because each step above costs a few minutes, and the pump question costs money. A plumber who arrives and reaches for a new pump before the aerator list is the man this article exists to protect you from.</p>"""),

 ("wall-fan-making-noise",
  "FIX",
  "Your ceiling fan is wobbling, humming or grinding: the five-minute triage",
  "Tighten before you balance, balance before you buy: the order that fixes most fan complaints with a step stool and a screwdriver.",
  """<p>The sounds a fan makes are a short vocabulary, and learning it saves a replacement: each one names a specific part that is loose, dirty or dying. The triage below works in the order the fixes cost - a step stool, then a hand, then money.</p>
<h2>Identify the voice</h2>
<ul><li><b>Wobble (the blades trace an ellipse you can see):</b> first - are the blade screws tight and the down-rod's little clevis pin at the ceiling hook seated properly? Most wobbles are a rod joint that has rattled half out, not bent blades. Then balance: a cheap clip-on weight set or a coin taped blade-to-blade until the run smooths tells you which blade is the light one, and a follow-up tightening of its screws is half of all "my fan needs balancing" jobs.</li>
<li><b>Hum at low speed only:</b> usually electric - the speed controller or, on older fans, the capacitor's frequency against the motor; if the hum climbs with the speed and the fan spins free by hand, the diagnosis is the capacitor (a shelf-cheap part any fan technician carries) and not the motor.</li>
<li><b>Rumble that changes when you touch the housing lightly:</b> mechanical looseness - the motor's retaining ring, the housing screws into the mounting bracket, or the light kit's glass - press-test each with one hand at the stick, and the noise follows the culprit; nothing else in the house diagnoses by <em>touch</em> as beautifully.</li>
<li><b>Grinding or rasping, every rotation:</b> dust-caked blades hitting the tolerance, a wire inside the cowl, or the motor's bushings running dry on older motors. Power off at the wall, spin the blades by hand: a gritty feel and you have the bushing verdict, and bushings are the part "my motor's dead" shops quietly replace for less than a new fan's tenth.</li>
<li><b>Squeak that follows the switch-on, not the speed:</b> the mounting bracket at the ceiling box - the whole fan sags a millimetre when it spins and the bracket's screws creak. The fix is re-tightening with the bracket pushed up, so undo its ceiling-box screws, lift, tighten - and if the ceiling box itself is the flexing part, that is the one fan job to call someone for, because the ceiling box holding a spinning weight is the house's problem, not the fan's.</li></ul>
<h2>What to do while you're on the stool</h2>
<p>Dust the blades top and bottom with a pillowcase over each one (the cloth traps the harmattan load instead of the room's lungs - the same trick the harmattan-fire page recommends twice a month); look at the down-rod's cotter and the safety wire in the bracket for anything frayed; and with the fan off, give it a push and watch the coast: a fan that stops within a second or two twice in a row is telling you about the bearings before it stops politely mid-heat.</p>
<h2>Skip the theatre</h2>
<p>Balancing kits sold without checking the screws first; "silent" replacement fans bought while the old one's housing screws were never tried; and oil ports that modern sealed motors do not have - if the guide you read says to inject sewing-machine oil and there's no port on your motor, the advice is thirty years stale and the answer is the bushing or a new motor head, not a drip. The five-minute order - tighten, balance, clean, listen, then decide - resolves the majority of everything written here without opening a wallet.</p>"""),

 ("water-heater-no-hot-water",
  "FIX",
  "The shower is cold and the heater light is on: five checks before anyone quotes you",
  "Element, thermostat, cut-out, connection, tank - the water heater's fault ladder in the order a technician would climb it, with the safe version for you.",
  """<p>An electric water heater is a kettle with ambition: a tank, an element that is a stove coil in disguise, a thermostat that is a switch on a spring, and a cut-out that gives up before the fire does. Cold water means one of five things failed, and the ladder from simplest to final is worth knowing - because half of "element failures" are the cheap switch having done exactly its job.</p>
<h2>The five, in order</h2>
<ul><li><b>Isolation and breaker first, always.</b> The heater has its own switch on the wall or its own breaker; both get looked at before both get reset, and the panel gets sniffed for the warm-fishy smell from this desk's breaker article before any reset happens at all. Power off at the wall before touching anything else in this list - the honest version of "unplug it" for a hardwired box is the breaker, with the neighbours' lights off so you can see the tell-tale glow die.</li>
<li><b>The high-limit cut-out.</b> Inside the thermostat cover sits a reset button on most units: it fires when the tank overheats - which is what a dying thermostat does before it dies, and it is also what a heater does when it ran empty during a supply cut. With power OFF, remove the access cover (screwdriver, one panel), find the little red or black button, press it. Heater lives, hot water returns in an hour: the fault was the safety, and you still owe the unit a thermostat check - a cut-out that fires once was brave, a cut-out that fires monthly is a witness.</li>
<li><b>The thermostat itself.</b> Set to maximum as a test - a thermostat stuck below the cut-out never lights the element, and the dial being down is the cheapest cold shower in the trade. A multimeter continuity check across its terminals, power off, is five minutes for anyone who owns the meter; if the reading contradicts the dial, the part is the invoice.</li>
<li><b>The element.</b> The classic: continuity through the element should be a low, real number, and continuity from either terminal to the copper tank should be nothing at all. A reading between a terminal and the tank means the element's sheath has cracked and it is leaking current into the water - which is the failure mode that makes "the RCD trips in the bathroom" the last warning you get before the shower becomes a conductor. That one is not a DIY swap; that one is the electrician's call, tonight.</li>
<li><b>The tank, and the year it is at.</b> A heater past its element's designed life in hard water area throws the element, the anode and eventually the seam all in the same season. A weep at the flange, rust bloom at the seam, a tank that has clearly never been descaled in the house's ownership: quote the whole unit, not the part - this desk has watched people buy elements for a coffin.</li></ul>
<h2>The two minutes of prevention that cost nothing</h2>
<p>Set the thermostat to what the manual calls its lower band - a hotter tank scales faster and scalds the children's bath, and no comfort is purchased above "properly hot"; and if the area is hard-water, a drain-and-flush habit at the annual maintenance day moves the grit out before it becomes the element's burial shroud. The gas instant heaters get their own short line here too: no spark means the batteries first - the water-flow switch second - never the gas - and a flame that lifts off its burner is a shut-down-and-call fault, not a tune-up.</p>
<h2>What this desk will not pretend</h2>
<p>Tank draining, element replacement in a wet cupboard, and any work on a leaking energised vessel are jobs for the qualified person with the right tools and the insurance - the value of this page is that you hand them the diagnosis instead of the shower-curtain rumour, and pay for the repair, not the guess.</p>"""),

 ("toilet-weak-flush-fixes",
  "FIX",
  "The toilet runs weak, runs forever, or won't refill: the cistern is four parts",
  "Flapper, float, fill valve, jet ports - the cistern's small anatomy, the five-minute tests for each, and the two jobs that mean call the plumber.",
  """<p>Every toilet complaint in the house lives inside the tank, and the tank holds four working parts, which makes every complaint a four-question interview. Plumbers call a cistern "easy money"; the reason is that nine times in ten the part that failed costs a wallet-swap and the tenth is the job they genuinely need. The interview below is how to know which ten you are in.</p>
<h2>Four parts, four symptoms</h2>
<ul><li><b>The flush is weak or dead: the flapper (or the tower seal).</b> Lift the lid; flush while watching. The seal should lift fully and stay up the whole flush. If it drops early, the chain is too long (adjust to a slack thumb's width - the whole fix, free) or the seal has gone soft and black from the chlorine tablets everyone drops in here (replace it; it is the most stocked toilet part in any hardware shop).</li>
<li><b>The tank barely fills: the water level, set by the float.</b> The mark on the back of the overflow tube is the target - the level should sit about an inch below it. A float set low gives a weak flush even when every part works, and a float set high sends all the excess down the tube while the tank "won't fill." One bend or one screw, depending on the era of the mechanism.</li>
<li><b>It runs forever after flushing: the fill valve not shutting.</b> Listening at the lid: a hissing tank that never quite stops is the valve's seal dying - and while you are there, the refill tube aimed into the overflow (it should trickle a stream down the tube; if it's detached, the bowl never refills to its smell-blocking level - which is why "my toilet smells after the flush" is a twenty-second fix you can find here).</li>
<li><b>Bubbling, glugging, slow drain after a good flush: the rim jets and the trap.</b> The small holes under the bowl's rim are what create the swirl; mineral crust closes them into a dribble - an old toothbrush and vinegar down the ports, held wet with paper towels for an hour, is the treatment. A toilet that flushes clean-but-then-refills-noise-like-a-drinking-animal is a partly blocked vent stack: that's the roof, and that's the plumber.</li></ul>
<h2>The two where you stop and call</h2>
<p>Water at the base of the toilet after flushing (the wax ring or the flange is failing under the floor, and the subfloor is learning about it) and sewage smell that no amount of scrubbing fixes with the tank's refill and the trap's water level both verified good - that smell is the drain system telling you it needs a professional camera, not a bucket of bleach. Everything above the two is a lid-up afternoon.</p>
<h2>The habit that prevents half of them</h2>
<p>No chlorine blocks in tanks with rubber seals - they eat the flapper from the soft side, and the shop selling them will not mention it; flush only what the trap was designed for, because the "flushable" wipe earns its name only in marketing; and when the house will be empty for weeks, close the isolation valve behind the toilet and flush the tank nearly dry - a seal that dries under a full tank cracks, and that is the whole story of toilets found dead after every festive-season trip.</p>"""),

 ("paint-peeling-walls-bathroom",
  "FIX",
  "The paint is coming off the bathroom wall in sheets: why, and the sequence that lasts",
  "Trapped moisture, skipped primer, and the alkali burn from cement render - the three causes, the dry-wall test, and the redo order that actually sticks.",
  """<p>Peeling paint is almost never a paint problem; it is a moisture or surface problem wearing paint's clothes. The bathroom is the house's champion at both, which is why the same wall re-peels through three expensive repaints until the sequence below is done in the right order, with the right patience for the drying step that everyone shortens.</p>
<h2>Diagnose in three minutes</h2>
<ul><li><b>Where it peels tells you the cause.</b> Peel at the bottom third, near the floor or outside-wall base: rising damp or a shower leak finding the low path. Peel behind the shower wall on the other side: water is crossing the wall and the paint is the membrane it escapes through - check the grout and the silicone bead around the tray first (the ripple article's logic, one wall away). Peel everywhere at once after a holiday with the windows shut: the room lived on its own steam - the condensation article's territory - and the paint just kept the score.</li>
<li><b>The foil test, overnight:</b> tape a square of kitchen foil to the wall, tape all edges, check in the morning - water on the room side, condensation; water behind the foil, the wall is carrying it from outside or within. The two answers need different fixes and no amount of paint distinguishes them.</li>
<li><b>White crust with the peel (efflorescence):</b> that is cement's salts arriving with the water and staying when it dries. It is the one that specifically punishes painting through: paint over salt and the new coat inherits the eviction.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The redo sequence, no shortcuts</h2>
<p>One: fix the water - the leak, the grout, the ventilation - or delete this list from your plans because you are repainting a clock. Two: scrape every loose edge to firm paint, sand the feather, and - this is the step everyone skips - wash the whole panel with detergent, rinse, and let the wall dry on its own for at least several days with the windows open; a wall that is dry to the paint tester's cheap meter can still be wet behind the render and the meter's numbers are what to watch, not the calendar. Three: prime - and the primer here has a name to ask for: an alkali-resistant or stain-blocking primer on cement render (ordinary emulsion on fresh render is eaten by the same salts above, and "it drank the paint" is that conversation), and a proper anti-mould primer where the bathroom black has lived. Four: two thin coats of bathroom-rated emulsion, not one heroic one, and the fan or window running for the week after - the coat cures against moisture, and a shower in a freshly painted box on day two is a shortcut that re-opens the cycle.</p>
<h2>What this desk won't pretend about ventilation</h2>
<p>A bathroom with no window and no extractor is a condensation machine no product fixes; an extractor door to the outside beats one dumped into the roof space, and a trickle vent kept open costs nothing and outperforms every "anti-mould paint" claim, which are sold as a coat and delivered as an excuse. If the peel keeps winning after a correct sequence done honestly, the wall is getting water you cannot see from a leak you have not found - that is the investigation this page sends you off to make, and the leak-detection people with their cameras are the last, right answer.</p>"""),

 ("solar-panel-care-nigeria",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "Your solar is producing a third of what it used to and the inverter isn't lying",
  "Dust, soot, shade and the hot-spot walk: the maintenance a panel actually wants, the cleaning rules that don't void anything, and the checks for the box in the sun.",
  """<p>Solar panels are famous for being maintenance-free, and the sentence everyone quotes skips the Nigerian addendum: dust, harmattan haze, cooking soot on the kitchen-side roof, and a mango that found its ambition at the panel's edge. A dirty panel loses output in silence - the system doesn't fault, it just earns less. This is the maintenance the marketing forgot.</p>
<h2>The output evidence, before any climbing</h2>
<p>Your inverter or charge controller is the instrument: log the same hour's production on three clear mornings, or read the app's history - a slow sawtooth decline with a rain-wash recovery is dirt; a hard step down after a storm is damage; the one-to-test is a partial shade pattern (a new shelf, the neighbour's antenna, the palm that was a seedling at installation). Dirt is a maintenance job; everything else on that list is a fault hunt. If the figures show nothing, the panel stays on the roof where the sun is keeping it honest and the inverter's history is the witness.</p>
<h2>The cleaning rules that keep the warranty intact</h2>
<ul><li><b>Rinse, then glide - never scrub.</b> Soft cloth or a sponge on a pole, mild detergent only where there is grease. The glass is glass, but the edge seal is the vulnerable part: never a high-pressure washer, which drives water past the frame into the laminate, and never a dry scrub of gritty glass, which sands the coating and the panel at once.</li>
<li><b>Cool glass, early morning.</b> Panels straight-off-the-shelf at midday can crack under a cold pour - thermal shock is the one thing that breaks panel glass with no fault to show - and a wet panel in full sun dries into fresh water-spot mineral marks, so the dawn session is the only honest one.</li>
<li><b>From the ground when possible; from the roof only when the roof is safe:</b> the desk will not talk anyone onto a ladder - if the panels are beyond the eave-reach, the cleaning is a professional job with harnesses, and a good cleaning visit costs less than a single fall costs everyone. Walk the roof edge, check the feet: any rust bloom at a mounting foot or a lifted rail after a storm wind is a structural call, not a cleaning item.</li></ul>
<h2>The five-minute electrical glance (eyes off, hands off)</h2>
<p>Look, do not touch: cable glands and any visible wiring for cracks, rodent chews and UV-brittle insulation - a chewed cable is not a "tape it" job, it is the DC fault that has started a roof fire; MC4 connectors and the inverter's DC isolator for a discoloured plastic (heat at a loose connection shows its colour before it shows its fault code); the inverter itself for its own ventilation - a box cooking in the sun room throttles its output, which reads exactly like dirty panels on the app, and the shade board or vent slot that fixes it costs the price of a piece of plywood.</p>
<h2>The annual list</h2>
<p>Wash at the harmattan's end; check the mount bolt torque when it is safe to reach (vibration loosens what the installer tightened); trim the shade at the panel line; re-run the same-hour three-morning test and compare with the last year's same-month figures; and read the battery room's smell and colour - a swollen battery case or a sulfurous tang means the whole page becomes moot because the battery person is now the first call, on the day, not the week.</p>"""),

 ("gate-motor-solar-light-care",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The gate motor and the solar security light: the small-service list that keeps both alive through harmattan",
  "Humming-but-dead gates, misaligned photocells, dim lights by January - the two outdoor devices live on the same three enemies, and a Sunday afternoon fixes most of it.",
  """<p>The gate motor and the solar pillar light are the house's two permanently-outdoor electrical devices, and they fail the same way for the same reasons: the sun cooks the electronics, the rain finds the cable entry, and the dust packs the moving parts. Both are serviced by one afternoon's walk with the same small kit - cloth, brush, a screwdriver, hose water, not the pressure washer.</p>
<h2>Gate motor: the four checks</h2>
<ul><li><b>Humming, no movement - the run capacitor.</b> The motor's starting capacitor is the part that loses its strength quietly; the gate stops, the buzz stays, and a push moves it free. A fan-repair shop tests and matches capacitors for the price of the call-out itself, and the matching list (microfarads and voltage on the label) is photographed in thirty seconds - that photo is the whole shopping trip.</li>
<li><b>Reverses for no reason - the photocells.</b> The eye-and-target pair by the floor is what makes the gate stop for a car; a dust film, a spider's Sunday architecture, or a millimetre knocked out of alignment and the gate "sees" an invisible car forever. Wipe the faces, sight the brackets (a phone camera at one end, the reflector at the other, the light on the motor board as the judge), realign, re-tighten.</li>
<li><b>The rack and the hinge line:</b> grit in a toothed rack is the sound of teeth dying - brush it, check every bolt on the bracket plates and the rack's fixings (the gate's own vibration walks them loose before anything else), and the swing gates' hinges take the same treatment with the grease nipple as the prize. A gate that drags at one point of its arc has a foundation, not a motor, problem - the post's concrete foot has shifted or the ground has moved with a drain washing under it, and that is the mason's job before the technician's.</li>
<li><b>Battery and board: the quarter-year glance:</b> the backup battery's terminals for the white or green bloom (clean, grease thin, retighten - the gate that "stopped working" is the voltage drop at a corroded terminal nine times out of ten at the end of the rains), and the board's little LEDs for the fault code the manual decodes; surge is the board's other killer - a proper earth at the controller cabinet is what makes a lightning season survivable, and if the cabinet's earth wire is decorative, that is the electrician's five-minute job on the next visit.</li></ul>
<h2>Solar security lights: the honest maintenance</h2>
<p>The panel face gets the cloth - a film of harmattan dust on a mini-panel is the difference between a full charge and a sensor that dims by 10pm; the lamp head's vent and PIR lens get the brush, because a wasp's nest and a dust lens are the two "the light is crazy now" faults before the electronics are ever accused. The sensor's false triggers - the cat, the shrub the wind moves, the parked car's engine heat - are aim-and-reach adjustments, not faults; most units carry the two dials (sensitivity, time) and the rule "aim low, across the path, not along it." The one real replacement decision: the battery. These lights charge a small lithium or NiMH cell every day, and after two harmattans the dim-by-midnight light is a battery, sold by theAh matching, not a new light - the solar-repair area stocks them and a solder tab swap is the whole job for the patient hands.</p>
<h2>What this desk will not promise</h2>
<p>Anything inside a sealed board, any gate that grinds from the gearbox itself, and the mains-voltage side of an installation with no isolation switch to prove the work was done right - those are the tradesperson's line, and the service list above exists to make sure you are paying them for the repair and not for the dust.</p>"""),

 ("musty-wardrobe-clothes-rainy",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The wardrobe smells musty the week the rains end: the six-week fix, not the spray",
  "Mould lives where the wall is cold and the air is still. The reorganise, the desiccant maths, and the wash routine that saves the clothes you already mourned.",
  """<p>Mustiness is not dirt, it is farming: a closed cupboard with a cold outside wall, clothes packed against it, and the humid weeks of the rains gave the wardrobe its own weather. Sprays perfume the farm and leave the crop; the fix is to change the climate, and the clothes have their own rescue sequence.</p>
<h2>Move the farm's weather, in order</h2>
<ul><li><b>Empty it on a sunny day and open everything:</b> every door and drawer, the house windows too, and the wardrobe's back off the wall where the cold lives - the eight centimetres an inch of air gap buys is the whole free fix, and the packed-to-the-back wall is the reason the back row of clothes always spots first.</li>
<li><b>Wipe all inside surfaces</b> with the detergent-water-and-rinse cloth, not bleach on wood - then dry it fully, fan or sun, before anything returns; and look while you are in there: the dark bloom under shelf edges and the corner joints is the mould from the condensation article wearing different clothes, and the wall behind the unit gets the same inspection - a damp stain there is the leak investigation the paint article already told you to start.</li>
<li><b>Desiccant, with the maths:</b> the chalk-pots and silica trays work until they don't - most saturate within a rainy season and then sit there as decor; the calcium-chloride tubs with the collecting dish are the refillable honest version, and the box of loose silica beads is for cameras, not cupboards. Two pots, one near the floor (cold air pools) and one mid-shelf, checked and replaced on the rains' first week.</li>
<li><b>Wash the wearable load</b> - not the wash-day pile, the whole season's rotation: whites on the hottest the label allows with the detergent dose for soiling (not the aroma dose for scent), colours at the warm-with-vinegar-half-cup add that the older laundry hands already swear by, and the sun is the actual anti-must smell agent: the UV and the dry heat end what the wash only loosened. The leather and the suede are the specialists' - the sun that saves cotton is what cooks leather into shell.</li>
<li><b>Return the clothes with the gap discipline:</b> nothing touching the back wall, the heavy coats at the front where the air moves, and the "it can wait" dry-clean-only pile out of the cupboard entirely - a garment bag in a still wardrobe is a greenhouse with a zip.</li></ul>
<h2>The rhythm that prevents next year</h2>
<p>Open the doors an hour on harmattan afternoons - the season's dry air is free and it is the only time the walls themselves release what they stored; a solar fan in a top vent is the serious version of the same sentence and costs less than the clothes the last season taught you to value. And the one-line habit with the best ratio of all: no damp anything into the wardrobe - the half-dry towel, the gym shirt, "it's basically dry" - every one of them is a crop rotation for the next must.</p>"""),
 ("sewer-smell-after-trip",
  "FIX",
  "The house smells of sewer after the long trip: it is almost always dry plumbing, not broken plumbing",
  "Traps evaporate, vents choke, and soakaways tell on you in heat. The five-minute water pour that fixes half of all odour calls, and the signs that mean it is not the traps.",
  """<p>A house that comes back from a trip smelling wrong has, nine times in ten, a plumbing system that simply dried out while you were gone - and the fix costs a bucket. Every drain in your home is guarded by a bend of trapped water called a P-trap: that little U of pipe under the sink exists to hold a plug of water between your nose and the sewer. Water is its only maintenance. Leave for three weeks and some of those plugs evaporate; the gas arrives through the empty bend like a guest with a key.</p>
<h2>The restoration pour, in order</h2>
<ul><li><b>Every unused fixture, a good slug of water:</b> sinks, baths, bidets, the guest toilet (flush twice), and - the one everyone forgets - the <em>floor drains</em>, the gully traps in the car port and the bathroom corners. A bucket with a cup, one pour per drain until you see the water go and hear the gurgle of air moving: that sound is the trap refilling and the house re-sealing.</li>
<li><b>The washing-machine standpipe:</b> the drain hose sits in an open pipe - if it dried and shrank from its socket, the smell is coming straight from there; push the hose back to depth and consider the cheap rubber grommet that seals that joint, sold at every plumbing counter.</li>
<li><b>Kitchen sink with a double bowl?</b> Run each side; the shared tail-piece trap under the drainboard holds less water than you think, and it is a dry-out artist.</li>
<li><b>Give it a day:</b> trap smells fade within hours once sealed; a smell that persists past tomorrow is the start of the next section, not a refill problem.</li></ul>
<h2>When it is the plumbing, not the water</h2>
<p>Gurgling from a distant drain when the toilet flushes means the system is pulling air through its own vents - a blocked roof vent (nests, a palm frond, a wasp city) is the usual suspect, and that is a ladder-and-camera call for a professional. A smell strongest at the manhole or the soakaway after water runs points to a blockage or a full tank with nowhere honest to go - the emptying schedule is the maintenance, and a soakaway newly wet and stinking in a dry week is the ground telling you it has stopped absorbing. Outdoor smell after rain with a septic tank means the field or pit is surfacing: that one is the vacuum truck's call today, not next month. And the one red flag on this desk's list: a smell that comes and goes with the wind at <em>one specific wall vent</em> is the building breathing sewer gas into the house - that is the licensed person's investigation, and the trap-pour section above is only the polite way to have ruled out the free answer first.</p>
<h2>The habit for the next trip</h2>
<p>Ask someone to flush the guest toilet weekly - the house's least-used fixtures are its odour capital - or before you leave, pour a tablespoon of mineral oil down each floor drain: it floats on the trap water and slows evaporation to a crawl, which is the old hotel trick, legal in every manual, and worth remembering when the season's trip runs long. Homes should smell of nothing at all; if they do, the bucket list above is the first, cheapest and most correct place to start.</p>"""),

 ("water-storage-safety",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "Stored water stays safe by rules, not by luck: the container, the lid, the rotation",
  "The four conditions that keep a jerry-can from becoming a culture flask, how to clean a tank that has seen seasons, and the signs that mean boil or treat, not hope.",
  """<p>Water is not the risky part of storage; the container is. Clean water in a dirty, sunlit, open vessel becomes unsafe on a schedule measured in days, and the household that treats storage like plumbing rather than provisioning is the one that never has to think about this page. The rules are few and physical.</p>
<h2>The four conditions</h2>
<ul><li><b>Food-grade and dark by nature:</b> the blue HDPE drum and the purpose-made storage tank exist because they do not leach and they block light; a translucent container on a sunny terrace is a greenhouse with a tap. The old engine-oil jerry can, however well rinsed, is permanently in the wrong: no scrub reaches the plastic's memory.</li>
<li><b>A lid that seals, and a draw that does not need hands:</b> a tap or a pump over a wide open neck. The single most common contamination of stored water is the cup - and the hand that held it, dipped in for the family. One dedicated cup, hanging outside the lid, is a culture war won cheaply.</li>
<li><b>Cool, and off the floor:</b> heat multiplies everything biological; a pallet or a stand turns a concrete slab's damp into nothing.</li>
<li><b>Rotation as policy:</b> the water you draw first is the water stored first - two drums labelled with their fill dates beat one drum trusted to memory. Week-scale storage wants a weekly top-up from the supply line; month-scale wants a plan (below).</li></ul>
<h2>Cleaning the tank that has seen seasons</h2>
<p>The honest sequence, doable in a morning with the supply off: drain fully; scrub the walls with a long brush and a detergent solution - biofilm is a film, it comes off mechanically, no disinfectant substitutes for the elbow; rinse until the suds stop; then sanitise - a weak chlorine solution (household bleach, a couple of millilitres per litre of clean water in a standard unscented brand - check your bottle) swished over every surface, or left to stand in a full drum at the same order of dilution, then a short contact time and a thorough rinse. The sediment at the bottom of a borehole-fed tank is a separate page: the same desk's kettle article covers what scale means and when it means the filter order needs attention.</p>
<h2>Boil, treat, or test - the line that matters</h2>
<ul><li><b>Boil:</b> any suspicion after a flood, a cracked lid, an animal's visit - a rolling boil for one full minute and cooling with the lid on is the whole science, the same instruction public-health bodies have given for a century.</li>
<li><b>Treat:</b> chlorine tablets designed for water, dosed to the tablet's own instructions, then a 30-minute wait - the correct answer for the long dry-season tank, and a stopgap that never replaces cleaning.</li>
<li><b>Test:</b> borehole or well water for the house, once a year, at a real lab - the microbiology and the nitrates are invisible, and this desk will not pretend a page substitutes for a plate count. If the household includes an infant, a pregnancy, or anyone immunocompromised, the clinic's advice outranks every rule of thumb here, including this sentence's neighbours.</li></ul>
<h2>One closing habit</h2>
<p>Date what you store, cover what you pour, and let the oldest water drink first. The family that does these three things will read this page as obvious - which is the only outcome worth writing about.</p>"""),

 ("the-battery-room",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The inverter's battery room: the ventilation, height and separation that keep it boring",
  "Charging batteries want air, dry floors and no live sparks nearby. The room tour that costs an afternoon and prevents the one fire this desk cannot write enough warnings about.",
  """<p>A battery room earns its fire statistics by being treated as a corner instead of an installation. Flooded lead-acid - still the workhorse of every Nigerian inverter system this desk has counted - produces hydrogen while it charges, sheds acid mist, and resents heat; and the inverter itself is a fan-fed electrical cabinet that wants the same three gifts: air, height and separation. Boring is the goal; boring is a design, not a mood.</p>
<h2>The ventilation rule</h2>
<p>Charging batteries vent gas - mostly invisible hydrogen, the flammable kind that wants a spark and a low ceiling. The fix is air at both levels: a permanent opening high on the wall (hydrogen rises and holds under ceilings and cabinet tops) and one low, for the acid mist that is heavier, and cross-flow means the openings are on different walls, not two on the same side pretending to be a draft. A room that is hot enough to sit in during the afternoon is hot enough to cook battery life off a chart - the same heat that makes you open the door is the thing stealing years from the cells, and a room with a working louvre and shade beats a sealed clean-looking box that nobody can breathe in. And no: an extractor fan that only runs when you remember it does not qualify as ventilation. If the charger's own cooling fans are sucking the room's air into a closed cupboard, the cupboard is the room's flaw.</p>
<h2>Height, floor and containment</h2>
<ul><li><b>Batteries off the floor, on a stand rated for their weight:</b> the numbers on an inverter battery are not phone batteries - each cell is a heavy block whose stand should look over-engineered. Concrete's damp corrodes terminals from below and a flooded floor meets the acid pool halfway; a ventilated raised platform is the whole requirement.</li>
<li><b>A floor that forgives spills:</b> tile, sealed concrete or a chemical-resistant mat under and around the bank - an acid splash on a painted wooden shelf is the slow fire nobody sees coming, and the terminal-corrosion white powder you can wipe today is the resistance problem that cooks a connection next year.</li>
<li><b>Separation, the one nobody prices:</b> the inverter, the breakers and the batteries in the same cabinet is the design until a charger sparks at the terminals and the hydrogen that has been politely pooling at the cabinet top is no longer polite. Distance or a vented partition between electronics and cells is cheap architecture; the manual's own words on the subject are the warranty's.</li></ul>
<h2>The tour, every month</h2>
<p>Sniff at the door (a sharp, pool-like tang is over-charging and it is a fault, not a flavour); look at terminals for bloom - clean, a film of petroleum jelly, retighten with the right tool, never a loose shroud over a hot joint; check water levels on flooded cells with the cap off and distilled water only, never top-up with the tap to be kind; feel each battery's case with a palm - one cell hotter than its siblings is failing, and that is the replacement conversation today, not after it swells. And the extinguisher near the door rated for electrical fires, visible, not expired, is not decoration: the room deserves the same plan the kitchen gets.</p>
<h2>Where the boundaries are</h2>
<p>Wiring, breaker sizing and any work inside the inverter itself belong to the licensed installer whose number should be taped inside the room's door; the battery bank's series connections carry lethal voltage even with nothing else switched on, and this desk will not pretend an article turns a homeowner into the right hands for those terminals. Everything above is the room; the current is theirs.</p>"""),

 ("ceiling-leak-11pm",
  "FIX",
  "The ceiling is dripping at 11pm: the night protocol, hour by hour",
  "Safety first, then the drain: cut the right power, punch the bulge, save the floor and the evidence. The list for tonight and the calls for tomorrow morning.",
  """<p>A ceiling stain that turns glossy and a drip arriving in the bucket's rhythm is one of the few home emergencies where the first ten minutes decide the size of the bill. Everything on this page is sequenced by the clock, because panic is just a schedule problem - and the schedule below is what a professional will wish you'd followed.</p>
<h2>Minute one: electricity</h2>
<p>Water above a ceiling meets wiring, fittings and light fixtures below it. Before the buckets, go to the panel and switch off the breakers feeding that room - the ceiling lights, the sockets, the fan - and check by the switch, not by memory. Standing water on a floor with a live cable's path through the joists is the second accident this situation can have, and the first one was not even yours yet. If the drip is at or near a light fitting, that fitting stays off permanently until a professional has it in hand: a downlight is the fastest route for water into a live circuit, and a torch is enough for the night.</p>
<h2>Minutes two to ten: control the water</h2>
<ul><li><b>The bulge is a reservoir:</b> a sagging, drum-tight patch of ceiling is holding water that gravity will release all at once, with the board, at the worst hour of the night. Punch it deliberately - a screwdriver from a wide stance over a bucket, one hole at the low point - and the water drains where you want it instead of everywhere. This feels violent; it is the difference between a controlled release and a collapsed panel.</li>
<li><b>Catch and channel:</b> the bucket, a cloth inside its rim to kill the splash-noise, a second towel under the drip's run-down-the-wall, and the floor protected with whatever plastic - a bag slit open outperforms any cloth because it does not wick onward.</li>
<li><b>Move what lives there:</b> furniture from under, electronics and documents up and out - the humidity of one night under a drip does more than you would believe to a console's board.</li>
<li><b>Stop the water above it, if that is you:</b> if the room above is a bathroom, no showers tonight, and the toilet's isolation valve (behind the pan, quarter turn) if the smell or stain says waste water; if it is the top floor and a storm is out there, the gutter's mouth gets a quick look from the safe vantage you already have - and no ladders at night.</li></ul>
<h2>Before bed: evidence</h2>
<p>Photograph everything while it is dramatic - the stain, the bulge mid-punch, the soaked insulation, the damaged contents - dated. Insurance claims and honest tradespeople both read photos better than stories, and the ceiling article on this desk's stain map explains why the pattern tells the source's name as clearly as the drip.</p>
<h2>Morning: the three calls, in the right order</h2>
<p>A plumber first if any water fixture is on that floor above the drip - the leak is supply or waste and it keeps leaking while you wait; a roofer or the slab-waterproofing person if it is the top floor and the stain appeared with rain; the electrician to certify the room's wiring dry and safe before you put the breakers back and the lamps on. The ceiling itself is last, always - the board, the repaint, the texture can wait for the structure to dry on its own schedule, and the one that dries a repaired ceiling too fast, with a heater or the sun's worst angle, buys itself a new crack by the next harmattan. Tonight you are a damage manager; morning is when the actual repair begins.</p>"""),

 ("harmattan-and-wood",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "Harmattan cracks wood: saving the guitar, the parquet and the door that suddenly closes",
  "The season pulls moisture out of every fibre in the house. The humidity maths, the cheap interventions that actually work, and the instruments that need the serious version.",
  """<p>Wood is a sponge with a grain, and harmattan runs the sponge on a cycle: dry air pulls moisture from the cells, the fibres shrink, and everything held together by fibre tension tells you about it - the guitar that will not stay in tune, the soundboard seam that opens a hair, the parquet's winter gaps, the door that closes by itself in September and sticks in March. You cannot stop the season; you can negotiate its terms.</p>
<h2>The number that matters</h2>
<p>Instruments and furniture live happily in the band around 40-55 percent relative humidity; harmattan pushes indoor air into the teens, and the damage is not a single night - it is the <em>duration</em> in the low band, measured in weeks. The honest way to run the season is to know the number: a small hygrometer costs less than a string set and tells you whether anything below is working. That single instrument is the entire first recommendation, and the desk has watched people buy humidifiers and never check whether they achieved anything - the hygrometer is how you know you did.</p>
<h2>The cheap interventions that work</h2>
<ul><li><b>A sealed room, a water surface:</b> a wide pot of water in the corner of the room you keep shut is a humidifier with no marketing; a damp towel over a chair back doubles it. The effect on a closed bedroom is real and measurable by the hygrometer within the hour - and it is a conversation with the season, not a fix, so aim the effort at the room that matters, not the whole house.</li>
<li><b>The instrument's case is the safe:</b> keep the guitar, the mandolin, the woodwind in its case, and a case humidifier (the sponge pill or the membrane pad sold for exactly this job) inside the case. The case's tight little box is where a small humidity source actually wins; the open stand in a harmattan room is where the crack starts.</li>
<li><b>Loosen the string, or the neck's argument:</b> an acoustic stored through the season at concert pitch under dry tension is asking the top to cup - the store-it-a-half-step-down advice from every luthier is cheap insurance, and if the action suddenly rises before you've played a note, the soundboard is telling you about its moisture, not its tuning.</li>
<li><b>Furniture and floors:</b> the polish-and-oil routine before the peak feeds the finish the season will drink; parquet gaps that appear are seasonal - do not chase them with carpentry mid-dry, the wood re-swells and closes in the rains, and a gap filled in January is a floor crowned in June. Door hinges that suddenly bite: a longer screw in the strike plate's top hole, or the planing this desk's fix-it pages already argue for after the year turns.</li></ul>
<h2>What the season's other damage list is</h2>
<p>The same dryness that splits a bridge tab cracks a plaster wall's hairline and shrinks a wooden doorframe's grip on its paint film; the dust from one is the fuel for the other, which is why the harmattan fire-safety page on this desk tells you to wipe the blades of every fan that runs, and why the electronics article tells you to open nothing without a plan for grit. Wood, paper, cloth and skin all take the same season; the number on the hygrometer is the household's one shared dashboard, and the house that watches it saves itself a string set, a luthier's opinion, and a door's dignity, every single year.</p>"""),

 ("borehole-pump-no-water",
  "FIX",
  "The borehole pump runs and nothing comes: prime, foot valve, or the air",
  "The three failures that make a running pump say nothing, the tests before the rig, and the moment the call stops being yours.",
  """<p>A pump humming with a dry delivery line is the most misdiagnosed fault in the compound - the generator man blames the pump, the neighbour blames the water table, and the actual answer is usually mechanical, cheap, and knowable in a morning from the ground. Here is the triage a good pump technician runs, in order, before anyone quotes.</p>
<h2>Fault one: it lost its prime</h2>
<p>A surface pump does not suck water from the sky - it pushes air out of the line until the water column falls into it, and it needs water in the pump and suction line to start. An air leak anywhere on the suction side - a joint loosened by years of vibration, a cracked foot-valve housing under the tank, a valve cover gasket dried to cardboard - and the pump recirculates air forever, warm and useless. The tests a homeowner can run: check the pump's priming plug is full after filling and re-fill after a minute of running (water that just disappears is leaking past the foot valve down the borehole - the diagnosis announcing itself); then, with the pump off, listen at the suction joints with the line pressurised by hand - the hiss is the answer, and a new gasket or a tightened union is the whole repair.</p>
<h2>Fault two: the foot valve has given up</h2>
<p>Down at the bottom of the rising main, a one-way flap holds the water column in the pipe between pumps so you never re-prime a full borehole - and when its seal fouls on grit or its spring snaps, the column drains back, the tank runs dry between uses, and the pump's "no delivery" is really a lost column. The signature: prime holds when the system's off, but an hour of rest and the sight-glass bubble test starts again. The honest repair is a pull - the rising main comes up on a tripod or a small rig, the old valve gets replaced, and the filter screens get cleaned while the pipe is out - a pump technician's half-day, and the price of the call is not the pump's fault.</p>
<h2>Fault three: the well has changed - the water table, the sand, the silt</h2>
<p>Seasons move the table; years sand it up. A submersible sitting in now-silted depths pumps mud then nothing, the flow that once ran strong now surges, and the recovery test - run until the tank's empty, watch the recharge time against last year's numbers - tells you the borehole needs a developer's surging or a deepening, not a new pump. This is the one branch where the call genuinely stops being yours: the rig, the bailing and the decision are the water-geologist's territory, and a pump swapped over a silting hole is money spent to be wrong loudly.</p>
<h2>The morning's order of operations</h2>
<p>Prime plug open, fill, wait, run - then the two-ear test on suction joints. If prime vanishes into the ground: foot-valve branch, call the pull-man. If the well ran down with you: recovery test with a neighbour's meter or the tank's own clock. If it is the third thing - surging, sand, the smell of the deep - the rig's visit and this page are both cheaper than a new pump that fixes nothing.</p>"""),

 ("kitchen-hygiene-routine",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The kitchen's invisible crossroads: the five habits that stop the board, the cloth and the fridge from passing germs around",
  "Most food trouble is a hand-off, not an ingredient: raw to ready, cloth to counter, warm to cold. The routine that breaks each hand-off, with the boundaries marked.",
  """<p>Food poisoning is mostly a story of transfers - a germ moving from where it lives to where it multiplies to where it is eaten - and in a home kitchen there are exactly four transfer routes worth building habits around. The habit list below is not fear; it is the short version of what a public-health inspection looks like from the inside of a small kitchen.</p>
<h2>Route one: the board and the knife</h2>
<p>The board that held raw chicken is not clean because it was wiped, and the same is true of the knife, the hands and the tap handle everyone grabs next. The two-board rule (one for raw meat and fish, one for everything eaten without further cooking) is the whole fix, plus the hot-detergent wash between tasks - bleach water after the raw board, and the board itself replaced when it gets deep knife scars, because a scarred board cannot be washed into honesty. The desk adds the storage note: the raw board lives below the ready board in the drain rack, so its rinse-water cannot drip onto the salad.</p>
<h2>Route two: the cloth nobody trusts</h2>
<p>A damp dish cloth at room temperature is not cleaning the counter, it is spreading what the counter had on it - the study-backed version of your mother's instruction to boil the cloth. The routine: one cloth for dishes, one for surfaces, a bucket of soapy water changed more often than you think, and the surface cloths boiled or bleached and fully dried daily. The old towel by the sink that "dries hands between everything" is the route this article is mostly about; it is where the transfer starts.</p>
<h2>Route three: the fridge's temperature lie</h2>
<ul><li><b>The dial is not a thermometer:</b> a fridge keeps food safe at cold enough, and the only way to know is a small thermometer on a shelf - the desk's fridge article explains why a dusty back and an overstuffed door make the dial a wish. Freezing the water bottle to test "is it cold" is folklore; the thermometer reading is the fact.</li>
<li><b>The two-zone rule:</b> raw meat sealed on the lowest shelf (drip is a transfer route too), ready food up top; nothing warm goes in - a hot pot in a small fridge raises the air temperature for everything else for an hour, which is how a fridge full of leftovers becomes the second course.</li>
<li><b>The four-hour line:</b> perishables sitting above fridge temperature for four hours are at the decision point - eat now, cool now, or the bin is the cheapest option in the whole food-safety ledger. This is the page's hard rule; it is also the rule no household honours during a party, which is when the arithmetic matters.</li></ul>
<h2>Route four: the water and the hands</h2>
<p>Safe water in the house is its own desk page - the routine here is about hands: soap and a real rub before handling food and after the raw board, the tap scrubbed to the wrist if a tap handle was gripped with a chicken hand, and the washing-up done so the drying rack - not a cloth - finishes the job. And the boundary this desk draws plainly: anyone in the kitchen with a cut, a boil or a stomach bug does not cook for the house - that is not judgement, it is the same transfer arithmetic as everything above.</p>
<h2>One line for the fridge door</h2>
<p>Germs travel by hands, boards, cloths and warm air; four habits, and the kitchen's invisible crossroads stops being an intersection.</p>"""),

 ("fridge-not-cooling",
  "FIX",
  "The fridge is running but the freezer is sweating: the four checks before you buy a new one",
  "Dust on the back, a door seal on strike, an overstuffed shelf, and the thermostat's misunderstanding - the four ordinary answers, then the two that need the technician.",
  """<p>A fridge that is "not cooling" is nearly always cooling badly - and the four reasons for badly are checkable this afternoon with a torch, a sheet of paper and a brush. The order below is by how cheap each fix is, because the replacement is the last thing on anyone's honest list.</p>
<h2>Check one: the coils and the room</h2>
<p>The grille at the back or below the door is the fridge's radiator, and its job is to dump heat out; a coat of dust turns it into a blanket - the compressor runs longer, the inside stays warmer, and the meter climbs. Unplug, brush and vacuum the fins, keep the unit off the wall (the air needs the gap), and look at the neighbours: a fridge sandwiched beside a hot generator wall or under a window's direct afternoon sun is fighting a temperature war the manufacturer's design never planned for. A moved fridge is a cooled fridge; the move is the repair.</p>
<h2>Check two: the door seal</h2>
<p>The rubber gasket is the fridge's electricity bill and its humidity barrier. The paper test - a sheet left in the door should resist a pull out - finds the honest answer fast; and the torch-in-the-cavity night trick (a phone light inside, see it glowing around the door edge) shows the leak's exact mile marker. A warped seal is either cleaned (food film on the lip keeps it from seating), renewed (model gaskets exist; the number is on the seal or the back sticker), or - on old units - coaxed back with warm water and patience. The freezer's own worst case: a frost ridge under the gasket means the seal has been losing air for weeks, and the defrost that follows is the reset, not the fix.</p>
<h2>Check three: stuffing, and the air's route</h2>
<p>Cooling is circulation: the vents inside the box must stay clear for the air to return, so a fridge packed like a suitcase cools its door shelves warm while the back freezes. The fan-forced freezers with the little vent grille inside are the worst offenders for "everything at the front is soft" when someone blocks that grille with the carton wall - rearrange before you suspect the machine. And the thermostat argument: colder settings are not faster settings; the fridge runs to temperature, and a dial cranked to max on a hot day mostly buys a freezer that ices up and a fridge that sweats.</p>
<h2>The two that belong to the technician</h2>
<p>Compressor hot, fans turning, coils clean - and the box still warm: that is the sealed system, refrigerant or the compressor's own valves, and this desk will say what every honest repair person says: a regas on an old unit is money loaned to a machine near the end of its loan period, and the quote should come with a replacement comparison, in writing, both numbers. And the buzzing that starts and stops every few minutes like someone is trying to wake it: a dying start relay or compressor - unplug it tonight, because a stuck compressor is a fire's patient candidate, and the smell of hot enamel is the page's one emergency line.</p>
<h2>The habit list</h2>
<p>Back-brush each harmattan-end; the paper test every quarter; hot food cools on the counter before it goes in; and the fridge full of one month's cooking oil and three bottles is the fridge that works - air needs somewhere to go. These four lines are the difference between the appliance that lasts twenty years and the one the market sells twice.</p>"""),

 ("ceiling-fan-mounting-right",
  "FIX",
  "The ceiling fan that wobbles is telling you about its mount: the three-part box that holds it",
  "A fan is a spinning weight on a ceiling; the box, the rod and the brace are the engineering. What a safe mount looks like before you switch it on, and the sounds that mean stop.",
  """<p>Every dropped fan in every video starts the same way - not a bad fan, a bad box: a lightweight wall-box screw holding a bracket that was never designed for a machine that pulls on its own mount at every rotation. This is the ceiling-fan mounting page that pairs with the noise article: the wobble is the symptom, the mount is the cause, and the fix is done once, at install, on a ladder, before the blades spin.</p>
<h2>The three parts that carry the load</h2>
<ul><li><b>The box:</b> the fan must land on a ceiling box stamped for fans (the words "ceiling fan support" or a weight rating are moulded into metal boxes for a reason) - and in a Nigerian concrete or timber roof, that means the box is fixed to the joist, rafter or a timber plate bolted to the structure, not to the board alone. A box on plastic anchors in plaster is the mount that fails next harmattan, not next year.</li>
<li><b>The brace:</b> between the structure and the box sits the real engineering - a brace or a mounting bar with through-bolts, the fan's mass travelling into the building's frame rather than a sheet of ceiling. For new installs this is the installer's conversation, and it is the desk's one firm sentence on this page: the ceiling box is the part you pay for once, in the installation, or pay for later in a room's panic.</li>
<li><b>The rod and the pin:</b> the down-rod's clevis hooks into the bracket's ball, and the little cotter pin plus the rubber isolator is what stops the whole fan's weight sitting on the wiring. The pin is the part everyone forgets and the part the drop-tests hinge on - the fan should hang on steel, and the wires inside the box should be slack enough that nothing they plug into is load-bearing.</li></ul>
<h2>The pre-switch checklist, from the floor</h2>
<p>Blades on and tightened (the screws are matched to the blade; a mismatched pair walks loose within a week), the canopy hiding nothing you would want to inspect, then hands-on: two palms on the housing, the fan on at max for thirty seconds - the housing should feel of one mind with the ceiling, no walking, no ring. The noise article's balancing steps handle a wobble of centimetres; this page exists for the wobble of the whole fan, which is a mount calling for a ladder and a box swap, tonight.</p>
<h2>What the desk will not approve</h2>
<p>A fan hung on a light fitting's own box (those are made for bulbs, not spin), a box screw that spins in crumbled plaster, the extension of the mounting rod done with anything other than the manufacturer's rod (the rod is a load path - a hardware-store pipe is a joke the ceiling learns about), and any install where the earth wire is the one without a decision. Fans are the safest appliance in the house when mounted like engineering and the least forgiving when mounted like a lamp - the difference is one bracket and the honesty to check it before the room sleeps under the blades.</p>"""),

 ("gutters-and-downpipes",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The gutter is the cheapest roof repair you will never do twice: clear it before the rains",
  "One blocked channel finds every crack in the paint, the eaves and the foundation. The half-day route, the slope trick, and the downpipe's one real rule.",
  """<p>On a Nigerian roof, water that misses the gutter has a longer to-do list than anyone likes: the eaves soaks and the ceiling learns the stain map, the wall's fresh paint delaminates from the damp below the soffit, and the ground under the foundation gets the whole roof's yield at its feet, where the flooding article's story already warns it is mining. The gutter is the one system that does all four preventions at once, and its entire maintenance is a half-day, twice a year.</p>
<h2>The half-day route</h2>
<ul><li><b>Clear, from the downpipe up:</b> gloves, a trowel or hand for the channel's mulch (the harmattan dust plus last year's leaf litter makes a felt that holds water like a sponge), and the downpipe's mouth opened first - a clear throat before the sweep, or the cleanings just relocate the blockage.</li>
<li><b>The hose test, twice:</b> flush from the far end toward the outlet - the water should run, not pool; then a full bucket at the far end and watch the line: a slow section is a low section, and a standing pool in a channel meant to drain is the sag that starts the rust or the crack at the joint.</li>
<li><b>Slope, the honest fix:</b> gutters drain by fall toward the outlet - a re-hung bracket line at the correct grade (a few millimetres per metre, the rule of "a drop the eye can see over the run" is the practical version) solves the pond that cleaning alone never will. The blocked end cap that weeps at the seam is the same story from the other side.</li>
<li><b>Joints and the lip:</b> sealant at every lap and end-cap, the fascia board behind the gutter checked for the soft patch (the leak that lives behind the metal), and the gutter's outer lip lower than the shingles' drip line - the one geometry error that guarantees the wind-driven rain goes behind the channel and into the wall, which the pre-rains checklist already tells you to watch from the ladder.</li></ul>
<h2>The downpipe's one rule</h2>
<p>Take the water away from the house. A downpipe that dumps at the foundation is a gutter you installed to concentrate damage - the elbow and the extension (a length of pipe, channel or the buried drain it used to feed) should land water where the ground can take it: away, down, and away again, which is also what protects the wall's render from the splash-up that paints a house's base a darker grey than its top. The chain that ends in a soaked subfloor and a termite invitation is this sentence.</p>
<h2>Two extras that cost nothing</h2>
<p>The gutter is the ladder-day for the free roof inspection the same article keeps demanding: look along the sheet seams, the ridge caps and the parapet's waterproofing while you are at the height; and at the end of the rains, do the route again, once - the second half of the year's leaves are the blockages that find you in harmattan with a channel full of compost. The whole of home maintenance is a few loops repeated twice a year; this is the cheapest loop on the calendar.</p>"""),

 ("gas-cylinder-safety",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The gas cylinder in the kitchen: the smell, the soap test, and the two habits worth the whole page",
  "LPG is heavier than air and goes where water goes. Storage, the leak's real test, the swap-day checks, and the emergency sequence that has no switches in it.",
  """<p>A cooking-gas install is a pressurised system with one weak link - the hose and its clips - and the household that respects that fact never thinks about it again. The page below is the whole of what respect looks like: four checks, one storage rule, and the emergency sequence that is the opposite of every instinct.</p>
<h2>The four checks, quarter by quarter</h2>
<ul><li><b>Soap, on the joints, with the burner on:</b> the honest leak test - washing-up liquid and water, brushed over the regulator's neck, both hose clips and the cylinder's valve spindle - and the bubbles are the answer. A candle or a lighter is never the leak test; it is the consequence. And a smell of gas in the room with nothing bubbling is the hose breathing or a valve not shut: open the window, and the sequence below applies to a smell you cannot place.</li>
<li><b>The hose itself:</b> rubber gas hose hardens and cracks from kitchen heat and time, and the "it still smells fine" hose is the one the repair shop replaces by the metre without being asked. The mark on the hose (a gas-rated line, the standard's print) is the purchase's only honest label, and the spring clips - not cable ties, not friction - are the fittings. If the hose touches the cooker's back where the heat lives, it is the length of the failure; reroute or shorten it to clear.</li>
<li><b>The regulator and the knob:</b> the correct regulator for the cylinder's valve (the shop's fitting, not the neighbour's), the valve off at the cylinder when the burner is off at the end of cooking - the two-switch habit, and the one that ends more incidents than any other, because a leak with the cylinder on is a leak with no expiry.</li>
<li><b>Swap day, three minutes:</b> the new cylinder's seal ring and the smell of the first light-off - the first flame after a change is the test of the seal, and the "it puffs then lights" is a leak into the housing; if it puffs big, stop, and the dealer's man is the fix. The cylinder stands upright, on a floor that drains, not in a cupboard that traps.</li></ul>
<h2>Storage: the down-and-away rule</h2>
<p>LPG is heavier than air: a leak does not rise, it walks the floor, finds the drain, the doorway's gap, the pit below the kitchen - and waits. So the spare cylinder lives outside, upright, in shade, with its cap on and a floor that lets it go away from the house rather than under it; and a kitchen where the cylinder must live gets the ventilated cabinet and the floor-level vent, the two features the gas installer's own rulebook already requires. The desk will say the flat-out version of the industry's advice plainly: a cylinder in a room that sleeps people is a decision about what happens at 3am.</p>
<h2>The emergency sequence - no switches</h2>
<p>Smell gas: hands off the electrics (a switch's spark is the ignition nobody thinks about), open everything that can open, kill the cylinder's valve if the walk to it is comfortable, leave and let the room air, and call - the supplier or the fire line - from outside. A fire at the cylinder: if you can shut the valve, you can put it out; if you cannot, leave and let the gas burn out in the open, because a capped leak finding an ignition is the second event no extinguisher answers. These are not dramatic sentences; they are the two lists the whole industry runs on, and they are why this page exists.</p>"""),

 ("tank-overflow-pump-dry",
  "FIX",
  "The tank that overflowed at 4am - and the pump that ran itself dry: two faults, one float",
  "The ballcock that gave up, the overflow pipe you should be grateful for, and the dry-run trip list - the water-storage faults that save you money when you notice them early.",
  """<p>On a day when the supply ran late and the pump ran on, most households learn about their water system in one afternoon: the road outside flooded with the tank's entire holding, or the pump's burning smell behind the door. Both events are the same two components - the float valve that stops the fill and the flow that keeps the pump's guts cool - and both have a small repair, a habit, and an explanation worth the whole page.</p>
<h2>The overflow: the system's own witness</h2>
<p>The overflow pipe is not a fault; it is the float valve's alarm, and the flooded compound is the sound of the alarm being obeyed. The float's job: when the water lifts the ball to its set height, the valve seats and the fill stops - and it fails three ways. The seal at the seat fouled with grit (the borehole's own silt doing what the kettle article describes from the other end - a clean and re-seat fixes it that morning); the float set too high or holed with water inside, so it never lifts (replace, and set the level below the pipe's crown - the gap is the whole design); or the valve's arm bent and jammed, which is the post-storm failure and the reason the lid is inspected after every violent rain. The two-minute test, with the tank nearly empty: lift the float by hand - the valve should shut the flow with no drama and no weep. That single test is the entire overflow article if you only keep one line.</p>
<h2>The pump that ran dry</h2>
<p>A centrifugal pump moves water and is cooled and sealed by the water it moves; with the suction dry, the mechanical seal runs hot in minutes and the impeller's clearance changes for good - the "it pumps weak now" after a dry-run is the seal's resignation. The fault list, and their fixes, in order of the money they save: the suction strainer blocked (the well's sand, the tank's debris - clean it, and the reason it was blocked); the foot valve stuck with grit so the line drains back between runs (the prime-loss article on this desk covers the same valve from the borehole end); a leak on the suction side letting air in so the pump never fully fills (the hiss at a joint in the primed system is the diagnosis, and a new gasket or union is the whole repair); and the switch - the pressure tank's control or the float's own - that kept the pump calling for water it did not have, which is where the dry-run protection belongs and where most installs skip it: an inexpensive pressure switch with a no-flow cut-out is the device that makes the dry-run a five-second incident instead of a replacement.</p>
<h2>The habit list that ends both faults</h2>
<p>The lid is on, always (the mosquito article's breeding point and this one's grit point are the same lid); the float lift-test once a month at the low water point; the strainer cleaned each time the borehole runs yellow; the pump's stop-start in a quiet hour listened to like a doctor (the short cycle that repeats is a pressure tank's air charge or the switch itself - both cheap, both urgent before the second season); and the overflow's exit watched, because a trickle on the compound after the tank is full is a float not seating, and a float not seating is ten thousand litres and the neighbour's side of the wall. Water systems keep score in litres; the page's whole argument is reading the scoreboard while the water is still inside.</p>"""),

 ("mattress-humidity-care",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The mattress is the dampest thing in your bedroom: the dry-out routine",
  "A bed on a solid base breathes once a day or it stores the whole night's water. Strip, stand, sun and rotate - the quarterly four steps, plus what mould under a mattress actually means.",
  """<p>A sleeping human hands the mattress a litre or two of water vapour a week - the reason a mattress in a closed Lagos bedroom smells faintly sweet by the rains even in a clean house, and the reason the routine below is the least glamorous, most effective hygiene in the home. The mattress is not dirty because you are; it is damp because physics is, and the fix is air.</p>
<h2>The quarterly four</h2>
<ul><li><b>Strip and stand:</b> on a bright dry morning, take off the sheets and - the step everyone skips - stand the mattress on its end against the wall, bare side out. A mattress lying flat dries its top and drinks its bottom; vertical, both faces see air at once. An hour in a cross-draft room does more than any spray.</li>
<li><b>Sun, in courses:</b> a true hour of direct sun on the fabric is the old remedy with real evidence behind it - UV at the surface and heat in the fibres do the deodorising no product matches. But the modern foams resent long baking: two short sessions a year, memory foam in the shade-and-air version of the same day. The sun also fades and dries the cover, and a faded cover cracks - the goal is dry, not brown.</li>
<li><b>Rotate - and flip if it allows:</b> the body's weight carves a valley where it sleeps, and the valley holds the damp; a quarter-turn each season (and a top-to-bottom flip on double-sided models, which most newer foam singles are not - read the label's own diagram) keeps the surface even and the drying faces alternating. The valley that already exists is a signal to raise the frequency, not to buy a topper over it.</li>
<li><b>Vacuum the seams, then the frame:</b> the piping seam is where skin dust collects and where the first grey line of surface mould declares itself; the vacuum's crease tool along every edge takes thirty seconds, and the frame - the slats or the board the mattress sits on - gets the same pass underneath, because the frame's damp is the mattress's damp, one layer down.</li></ul>
<h2>What the smell under the bed means</h2>
<p>The must that hits you when you strip a mattress that has been on a solid base for months is the base's fault as much as the mattress's: a sealed platform blocks the underside's only air route, and the result is the cold condensation the concrete article warns about, at sleeping height. The two fixes the furniture makers will not advertise: slatted bases with real gaps, and - when the bed frame is what it is - the standing-airing routine above becomes the whole maintenance, not an extra. A dark spot that stays after drying, with a fuzzy edge and a smell that returns within days of cleaning, is established mould - and at that point the honest advice is the mould article's: a mattress is porous, deep growth in foam is not a surface job, and the safe decision on a heavily affected mattress is replacement and the frame's clean-up together, not a spray and a hope.</p>
<h2>The three habits that end most of the four</h2>
<p>A protector with breathability on the label (the cheap vinyl type that "keeps the mattress new" keeps the water in the mattress, which is the opposite of new), the bed-making discipline of leaving it open for an hour after waking - the sheets folded back while the bathroom happens is free and it works - and no bed under a window's condensation line with the curtain pooling at the frame. The bedroom dries the house that dries you; the mattress is simply the thing closest to the proof.</p>"""),

 ("floor-drain-backflow",
  "FIX",
  "Water coming up the bathroom floor drain is a message about which pipe - and it names the side",
  "Your blockage or the street's: the one-room test, the two-way valve reality, and the decision tree between a plunger, a snake and the drain truck.",
  """<p>Water returning through the lowest drain in the house is the plumbing system's overflow speaking, and before panic, before the bucket line, before the phone call, the situation deserves a diagnosis in minutes - because a backflow from your own room's blockage is a plunger job and a backflow from the street main is the same plunger doing nothing while the real problem waits underground with your neighbours' water in it.</p>
<h2>Two minutes to find the side</h2>
<ul><li><b>The other-fixture test:</b> run the basin and the toilet. If they gurgle, drain slow, or push the same smell up the floor drain while you do it, the blockage is below them, in the shared tail of your house - your side, and the snake's job. If they run perfectly clean while the floor drain still rises on its own, your pipes are fine and the water is coming from the direction of the street - main side, and your plumber's cable is for the trap, not the road.</li>
<li><b>The timing tells:</b> your-side backflows answer to use - they happen when the bathroom is busy. Street-side backflows answer to rain, or to a neighbour's flush at any hour, and they happen when the weather or the road is the schedule; a gully outside that stood full for a day before the floor drain rose is the same sentence in the other direction.</li>
<li><b>The smell of the water itself:</b> clean-ish soapy water says the house's own shower line, backed up at a trap or a bent pipe; the grey-black and septic tells you the main is the one on strike. Nobody should confirm that with a hand; the eyes and nose are the instruments and that is the whole test.</li></ul>
<h2>What to do with each answer</h2>
<p><b>Your side:</b> the floor drain's trap is the usual suspect - hair and soap felt packed at the bend. The cup plunger with the overflow sealed works the honest way; a hand auger past the grate finishes the job when the plunger only moves the plug around, and the bathroom article on this desk that maps the cistern applies its same logic here - the cheap tool and ten minutes beat the call-out. The smell that remains after clearing is the biofilm on the pipe walls: a bucket of hot detergent solution chased with the hose from the open end is the reset, and monthly thereafter if the house has trees on the line.</p>
<p><b>Street side:</b> you have three rights and one duty. The duty: lift nothing and dive into nothing - main-blockage water under a house is a health exposure and a trapped-gas environment in one, and this desk says it plainly: the septic and drain professionals with the vacuum truck are the only hands for this. The rights: the estate's drainage is someone's maintenance line - the blockage at the manhole between you and the road is reported with a date and a photograph the same day; a repeated street-side backflow is an argument with the block drains and the local office, and the estate whatsapp is the beginning, the written complaint is the record; and where the house's own floor level is below the gully, the one device that changes the physics is the non-return flap on the house's exit - the one-way valve a good plumber fits at the last manhole before the road, and whose trade-off (you cannot drain fast while it is shut) is a decision to make in a dry month, not in the rising water.</p>
<h2>The before-the-rains line</h2>
<p>Every house whose street floods once a year gets this sentence from this desk whether it asked or not: the test above is for the incident, but the routine is for the season - gullies cleared, the manhole's cover off and its screen checked, the house's exit valve inspected seated - one Saturday in June keeps the whole August conversation from happening indoors.</p>"""),

 ("how-to-paint-a-room-right",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "One weekend, one room: the painting sequence that survives its first rainy season",
  "The job is prep and drying, not brushwork - the six-step order, the harmattan window, and the two cuts (tape lines and corners) the finish lives on.",
  """<p>A room painted badly costs paint; a room painted in the wrong weather or the wrong order costs the paint job on top of the room - the peel two rainy seasons later is in this article's middle, not the tins. Six steps, and the patience is in the ones people skip.</p>
<h2>The sequence, in order of skipped</h2>
<ul><li><b>One - the room is empty or centre-stacked and clothed:</b> plastic over what stays, drop-cloth on the floor (a dust sheet over carpet is a mop later), and the doors that will not be used closed and taped. The paint you do not clean off frames is the time you thought you saved.</li>
<li><b>Two - the wall, honestly:</b> wash the grease and harmattan film down (sugar-soap strength, rinse, dry), scrape every flake already leaving, sand the feather edge where old meets new, fill the holes and nail-pops and let the filler actually cure, then the dust off with a damp cloth and dry hands. A painted wall is a photographed wall after; the paint is the last thing that can hide a flaw, which is why the prep is the job.</li>
<li><b>Three - the prime that is not optional:</b> patches drink, and an unpainted filler spot shows through the first coat as a dull matte ghost - spot-prime the repairs and, on fresh plaster or a colour that is changing family (dark to light, gloss to emulsion), the whole wall in primer. The tin costs little; the two extra top coats you skip because of it cost the same tin twice.</li>
<li><b>Four - the cut:</b> corners, the ceiling line, the frames - a brush first along every edge in a loaded-but-not-dripping state, the shoulder of the brush doing the line, your wrist resting on a stepladder not hovering. Tape only on edges you cannot control, and pull tape while the last coat is still tacky, scored with a blade - tape left to cure glues the paint to the wall's future in a jagged signature. Two-handed people use a guard; the desk uses a steady hand and a low price on mistakes.</li>
<li><b>Five - the roller, two thin coats, and the drying that means drying:</b> medium nap for textured walls, the W or M pattern worked out while wet, the second coat after the time on the tin - not after the time you think - and the room ventilated but not draughted to the point the paint skins unevenly. Thin coats are not a technique, they are physics: thick paint dries from the skin in and stores its own wrinkle.</li>
<li><b>Six - the weather window:</b> the harmattan is the country's paint season for a reason - low humidity lets the coats cure at their rated time, and the dust is managed by the closed-door washdown routine instead of fighting a wet film. The rains are not paintable outdoors, and indoors they buy you a peel at the skirting and a slow skin no brand's formula survives; the two honest calendar answers are the dry season's heart and the harmattan's end, and a room painted in the wet months shows its calendar for years.</li></ul>
<h2>The two lines that make it look professional</h2>
<p>Cut the ceiling line one brush-width inside the joint and let the ceiling's own paint do the last whisper - the razor edge people admire is the line where two soft edges meet cleanly, and the ceiling is the softer coat. And the frame: paint the woodwork first, the wall second, and overlap the wall's coat a hair onto the frame's enamel - the crisp break comes from the different finishes meeting, not from tape, and it is the trick that makes a room look like someone who knows did it.</p>
<h2>What the whole page reduces to</h2>
<p>A washed, filled, primed wall painted thin in dry weather is a job that lasts a decade and needs no skill beyond the sequence. The shortcut economy - skipping prep, prime, or the calendar - is a room that looks fine until the first long rain, and the peel on the wall is the receipt.</p>"""),

 ("grills-and-bars-rust-care",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The burglar bars are rusting from the bottom up: the half-day that stops the rot",
  "Steel grills fail where water sits, and the fix is a wire brush, zinc primer and enamel - in that order. The safety check hiding in the same afternoon.",
  """<p>Every window grill and door bar in the country is a painted steel sentence that the weather keeps editing: the film the builder sprayed at the factory holds for a few harmattans, then the bottom rail - where rain and wash-water pool against the frame - starts to bloom brown, and the rust goes into the metal while the paint above it still looks fine. A grill is a security device, so this is not a cosmetics page: it is a maintenance inspection with a paint pot at the end of it.</p>
<h2>Read the damage before you buy the tin</h2>
<ul><li><b>Surface bloom</b> - brown dust on intact metal, paint chalky: cosmetic, the whole page below fixes it in a day per window.</li>
<li><b>Blistered paint with a hard scale under it</b> - the steel is already section-lost under the scale; the section you worry about is where bars meet the frame and where the frame meets the wall: scrape, inspect the real thickness with a fingernail on clean metal, and a bar that flakes through to pitting at a weld is the welder's visit, not the painter's.</li>
<li><b>Swollen, cracked render or paint around the fixing points</b> - the bar's anchors are rusting inside the wall and jacking the mortar apart, the same story the leak article tells about hidden steel; that is a fix-and-re-render conversation, and repainting over it is a year of camouflage.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The half-day per window, in order</h2>
<p>Wire brush and 80-grit to bare, honest metal on every rusted run - the bottom rails twice, because they are the reason the last coat failed; a rag with thinners or detergent-and-water to strip the dust and the grease off before anything goes on; the flash-rust rule - primer within the hour, the sky counts as a damp day; zinc-rich primer (the grey cold-galvanising kind the market stocks for exactly this) on the bare metal, especially every horizontal surface and joint, and one good coat on everything else; then the enamel - exterior-grade, alkyd for the gloss-and-toughness trade the market trusts, two thin coats, second after the tin's time - and the desk's one unforgiving line: paint the ends and the undersides of the bottom rail, the surfaces the rain hits from behind, which is the face no one paints and the face the rust attacks first. And the weather: this job belongs to the harmattan, where the coats cure at their rated speed and the dust is managed by the same wipe-and-wait discipline the painting article preaches.</p>
<h2>The safety audit that comes free with the brush</h2>
<p>The grill is a security device and an escape route, and both die quietly with rust. Push each bar: a solid bar should not move at its fixing points, and a clicking or rotating one is pulling out of a softened hole - the anchor or the weld is the repair, today. Check the hinges and any quick-release on the emergency side: the bedroom bars that cannot open fast are the harmattan fire page's problem in a worse way, so the padlock-on-the-inside habit, the emergency key on the hook, and the oil on every moving steel joint belong on this afternoon's list. The desk's closing sentence is the only one that matters for the whole page: the grill rusts at its bottom, the paint protects at its first coat, and the inspection is a ten-minute favour to the metal that keeps the house's promises at the window.</p>"""),

 ("ceiling-plaster-sagging-repair",
  "FIX",
  "The ceiling sounds hollow and bulges like a bladder: plaster sag before it becomes a hole",
  "Gypsum versus cement plaster, the tap test that maps it, and the decision between re-fixing the skin and taking it off. Plus the one call that cannot wait.",
  """<p>A ceiling that drums hollow when you tap it, or shows a soft dome where the light's shadow draws a curve, is a ceiling whose skin has stopped being attached to its structure. It will fall - not all at once, probably not tonight, and the sooner the decision is made the smaller the repair bill, because a sagging plaster is fixable and a fallen one is a room.</p>
<h2>Know which ceiling you have, because the repairs differ</h2>
<ul><li><b>Cement/sand plaster on concrete slab</b> - the Nigerian workhorse: the "hollow" you hear is a delamination of a hard shell from the slab, often after a water event. The tell: the crack around a light fitting, a corner that has detached visibly, or a dome that moves slightly when you press a broom handle to it - gently, once, as a test, not as a hobby.</li>
<li><b>Gypsum board on metal or timber battens</b> - the drywall ceiling: its failures are screw-pop, taped-joint crack, and the soft black stain of a slow leak eaten into the board. The sheet that "moves like a membrane" when pushed is a sheet that has lost its perimeter of fixings, and it can be re-screwed while intact; a board whose face paper has gone mushy cannot - gypsum is a chalk sandwich and water is its only enemy that wins at room temperature.</li></ul>
<h2>The decision tree after the tap map</h2>
<p>Tap a grid of the whole ceiling with a knuckle and mark the drumy zones with pencil - the map is the diagnosis and the quote, and it is the one page the tradesman will thank you for taping to the door. Small zones under a fastening point (a screw can re-clamp the skin to the slab or the batten behind it): re-fix with the correct anchors - masonry screws with washers for cement skins, board screws into the joist for gypsum - and re-tape, re-fill, re-prime the patched line with the stain-blocker step the ceiling-stain article carries. Larger zones, or a skin that moves under the broom handle, or any ceiling sitting in water: the affected area comes down - cut out, safely, with the room emptied and the eyes and hands protected, because old plaster dust and its black mould tenants are a health page of their own - and the new sheet is either skimmed back over a sound parent or boarded fresh, which is the cheaper half of the comparison everyone wants and this desk will not fake: a replaster of a whole ceiling is priced by the metre and the moisture's damage, and the honest quote is always three numbers - the plaster, the cause of the damp, and the painting. If a quote offers only the first, it is a promise about a repeat.</p>
<h2>The call that cannot wait</h2>
<p>A ceiling that has visibly dropped below its line, or a crack that runs across it with a step at the edges (one side lower than the other), is not a plaster problem any more - it is the slab or the frame moving, and that is the structural conversation with the engineer: this desk has said all it is licensed to say, and the line from a sagging skin to a building's argument is the one page that should end with "call today," not "schedule." The rest is the painter's calendar.</p>"""),

 ("borehole-water-taste-smell",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The water tastes of metal and the bath has turned orange: the three borehole complaints, named",
  "Iron, sulphur, hardness - what each smell or stain actually is, which ones a filter truly treats, and the two signs that mean the lab, not the shop.",
  """<p>Borehole water arrives with a small vocabulary of complaints, and each one is a specific chemistry with a specific answer - the reason most households buy the wrong filter is that they describe the complaint as "the water is not right" and the shop sells them a general something. Three names and their stains, and the page that reads like a doctor's chart because the symptoms do the diagnosis.</p>
<h2>Iron - the metal taste and the orange stain</h2>
<p>The taste of a penny, the tea-ring that will not lift from the kettle, and the orange streak running down a white basin: dissolved iron, common as the geology it came from. It is mostly an aesthetic and laundry problem, not a health one - the honest first sentence - with the one caveat that iron bacteria ride the same water and build the slime that fouls a well. The treatment that actually works is the one the filter sellers skip the detail on: oxidise first, then catch - an aeration stage (even the old trick of letting the roof tank's inflow fall through air does a third of the job), or the Greensand/oxide-media filter that turns the dissolved iron into rust you can see in the tank before the tap, and backwashes to clean itself. A plain sediment sock softens the story but does not take iron out of solution - that is the product-class mismatch this paragraph exists to prevent.</p>
<h2>Sulphur - the smell of a struck match in the water, not the drain</h2>
<p>The rotten-egg complaint is usually the drain's bacteria (the water article's trap-clean paragraph handles that test - smell the running water, then the drain with the water off). When the water itself smells of it, hydrogen sulphide is coming from the well, and the same oxidising filters catch it; the household's extra note: a chlorine shock of the well by a professional ends the bacterial version, and the smell returning in a month is the well needing its silt and the screen dealt with, not a bigger chlorine dose.</p>
<h2>Hardness - the scale the kettle article already knows</h2>
<p>The chalk in the element and the soap that will not lather are the same calcium story; the bathroom's own answer is the kettle page's (vinegar, descale, do not wait for the flow to thin) and the whole-house answer is the ion-exchange softener, with its honest trade: salt, backwash water, and softened water is not for the drinking tap in any number of health contexts - the desk's standing line is soft the bathroom, drink the kitchen tap from a pitcher or the boiled line, and the two-tap plan is the version the households that do this properly live by.</p>
<h2>When the answer is the lab, not the tin</h2>
<p>Three signs skip every filter conversation: a salty or brackish taste (salinity - no domestic filter takes it out, the borehole itself needs re-thinking or the neighbour's soakaway has met your aquifer and that is a siting problem with a health tail), a chemical or fuel note at any strength (the surface has reached the water table and the source needs finding by the proper people - this is the desk's emergency line on a boring-sounding page), and anything new in a well that has always been quiet - the change in the water is data, and the plate counts and the nitrate figure are the whole annual-test story the borehole article already asks for. The summary for the shop, in the sentence that ends most wrong purchases: name the complaint, show the stain, then buy the machine whose media answers that name.</p>"""),

 ("window-weep-holes",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The water on your windowsill is coming in through the holes your frame is trying to drain",
  "Sliding windows are drainage systems, not watertight ones - when the weep holes clog, the track becomes a bathtub. The five-minute clear, and the mesh that keeps the clear from becoming a mosquito door.",
  """<p>Ask why water runs inside past a sealed-looking aluminium slider after a hard storm and the answer is almost never the glass, the brush seals or the frame joints: it is the tiny slots along the bottom of the outer track, designed to dump wind-driven rain back outside, doing it at maybe ten percent of their capacity because the rain has packed them with harmattan dust, paint overspray and the mortar crumbs of the fitting. Unblock those five minutes of work and the window returns to the design it was sold with; leave them and every heavier rain tests the same weak point harder.</p>
<h2>The system in one honest paragraph</h2>
<p>Sliding frames expect water in. Storm gusts force rain past the sashes into the track by design - the track is a gutter, and the weep holes are its downpipes, usually at the outer bottom edge of the frame near each end, sometimes under little snap caps or a slotted cover. When the holes run clear, the track drains within minutes of the rain easing. When they clog, the track fills to the lip and finds the path nobody designed: over the end weep, past the end plugs, under the sash and onto the sill - which is why the leak seems to be at the corner, where the standing water simply reached the corner first.</p>
<h2>The clear, and the two ways to botch it</h2>
<ul><li><b>Tool set:</b> a cocktail stick or fence pick, an old brush, a cup of water. Open the sliding leaf fully, clear each hole from inside and outside, brush the channel, then pour and watch: clean weeps drink the cup within a minute and show it leaving outside. The whole job is two minutes per window; do the whole house before the first hard storm of the rains and again after harmattan settles.</li>
<li><b>The silicone botch:</b> the single most common way windows flood is the fitting's own tradesman sealing the frame to the wall and running the same bead over the weep holes "for a clean finish" - or a paint job glazing them shut. Any weep hole you find under paint or silicone is opened; the wall joint is where the seal belongs, the holes stay as born.</li>
<li><b>Pressure-washer botch:</b> the compound crew hosing the facade at the closed window will drive a barrel of water past every seal in seconds. The squeegee-and-cloth version reaching the track is how facade cleaning and dry rooms coexist.</li></ul>
<h2>The trade-off nobody warns you about: the holes work both ways</h2>
<p>Open drains are open doors - to mosquitoes at dusk and to the small, fast cockroaches at night. The answer is not to close them but to screen them: narrow mesh offcuts or brush-seal strips pressed into the channel behind the holes, sold for exactly this, cut to span the slot rather than seal it. One honest housekeeping note: a screened weep needs the same twice-a-year clear or it clogs twice as fast - the mesh is doing its job by catching what the window would otherwise pass.</p>
<h2>When clean weeps still end with a wet sill</h2>
<p>Three suspects remain and each belongs to a different page of this desk: the ground or tile lip outside sits level or falls toward the frame, so the wall itself becomes the bath - regrade or add a drip channel to the outer sill; the water is a guest from above, a roof or canopy leak running down the cavity to appear at the window (the water-travels principle of this desk's ceiling-leak guide - chase it up, not in); or the sash brushes and end plugs are past their life, which is a parts job for the aluminium man rather than this page. The order of reading the bill, though, stays: holes first, because they are free, then falls, then parts.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Two appointments a year and a pick: pre-rains clear, post-harmattan clear, cup test each one; never paint or silicone over a weep hole, never screen one without scheduling it into the same appointment. Every flooded sill this desk has seen had a working drainage design that nobody maintained - the window did exactly what it was built to do; the house stopped helping it.</p>"""),

 ("soakaway-filling-up-signs",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "Your soakaway is not 'full' - the ground around it is tired: how to read a slow-draining field",
  "The night gurgle, the greener patch of lawn, and why pumping the pit fixes some years and none of the others: the two failures wearing one symptom.",
  """<p>Every compound with a pit behind the fence eventually hears the sentence: the soakaway is full. It is also, almost always, the wrong diagnosis - because the pit is not what fills. A soakaway is a storage chamber handing its water to the soil around it, and the soil, not the pit, is the machine that does the drinking. When that machine slows, everything upstream starts behaving like failure; and the fix is different for each of the two real causes, which is why one family pumps every year and the next moves the laundry tap and never pumps again.</p>
<h2>How the system is built to work - and what it is built to fail at</h2>
<p>Solids settle in the pit; the liquid seeps out through the walls and base into a soaking field of ground that quietly absorbs and polishes it before the water table is reached. Over years, the soil face develops what the trade calls a biomat - a gelatinous biofilm that is actually part of the treatment, until it thickens into a seal - and the field's thirst drops. Rainfall has a share: a field saturated for weeks absorbs slowly no matter how healthy it was, which is why the smell and the gurgle are seasonal neighbours. The pit silting with solids, by contrast, is a maintenance fact, not a failure: it is why pumps exist.</p>
<h2>The signs, ranked by what they tell you</h2>
<ul><li><b>The night gurgle:</b> drains glugging as the last bath empties mean the system is pulling air through liquid it should flow through freely - the field slowing down, heard through the house's traps. The earliest and most ignored symptom; a blockage downstream of the pit would shut things fully, not gurgly.</li>
<li><b>The lawn that outgrows its neighbours:</b> a patch over or beside the field running greener, wetter and warmer than the rest through the dry weeks is the field surfacing the work it failed to do underground. This is the sign that says soil, not silt.</li>
<li><b>The smell with no source:</b> septic air at the pit's vent or along its field line when nothing was just pumped - the system's pressure finding paths its design did not intend.</li>
<li><b>Slow drains house-wide:</b> the bath standing for minutes in a house with clean traps and no single blocked pipe says the whole field is backing up - the late sign, not the first.</li></ul>
<h2>The two failures - and the pump test that separates them</h2>
<p>Cause one is solids: the pit has silted toward the inlet, and a proper pump-out restores months to years, cleanly. Cause two is the field: the pit was pumped and three weeks later the wet patch and the gurgle returned - and the honest read is that the ground is tired, the liquid still arrives and nowhere goes. The separating test costs nothing: after a pump, watch how fast the pit refills from nothing but household use - a tank rising back fast is a field that has stopped drinking. And the trap this sentence exists to spring: pumping a field problem every year is not maintenance, it is paying the same invoice to hide one decision - reviving or replacing the field, which is a professional's siting-and-digging question, exactly the kind this desk does not let a Saturday answer.</p>
<h2>The load side: five habits that make any field live longer</h2>
<ol><li><b>Roof water is not soakaway water:</b> a downpipe feeding the field area (or the same trench) is the commonest self-inflicted drowning - gutters to a soakaway of their own or the garden's far end; this desk's gutters guide ends where the pit area begins.</li>
<li><b>Fats and wipes are field poison:</b> cooking oil and the "flushable" cloth leave the pit to coat the soil and accelerate the seal; grease goes in the bin, wipes in the bin, both without ceremony.</li>
<li><b>The washing machine is the biggest flow:</b> one long laundry session can push a day's household volume through the field in twenty minutes - spreading the loads is free; the soakaway's worst enemy is an efficient Saturday.</li>
<li><b>Bio-activators help the pit, not the field:</b> the bacterial additives are a reasonable silt-choke friend and a soil-seal nothing; dose with that expectation and no other.</li>
<li><b>Nothing above the field except grass:</b> carports, slab extensions and compacted walkways over the soak line strangle the air the treatment needs - the field is lawn or it is a problem.</li></ol>
<h2>The desk's line on urgency</h2>
<p>Slow, smelly and green is a decision to make this month. Effluent standing where children play, at the borehole's upslope side, or surfacing along the compound drain is not slow - it is a public-health failure, and the professional call, the keeping-people-away measure and the fixing of the source happen the same week. Name the diagnosis in the first sentence when you make it - pumped pit, or tired soil - and the honest quote you get will be the repair rather than the repeat visit.</p>"""),

 ("fridge-food-safety-power-cut",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The power-cut food maths: what a closed fridge really buys you, and the throw-out list",
  "The four-hour line every food-safety agency repeats, why the sniff test is a liar, and the frozen-cluster trick that turns one outage week into none.",
  """<p>The fridge is the only appliance in the house whose performance you can measure in hours of standing cold, and most households spend that budget blind - opening the door to check, moving the chicken, "just taking the ice out" - then trust the nose when the power returns. The maths of this is settled, published, and simple: a closed fridge is a cooler box you already own, and the rules below stretch its hours, spend them on the right food, and tell you with a straight face what to throw out without tasting anything.</p>
<h2>The published line, and what it actually means</h2>
<p>Food-safety agencies converge on the same figures, and the desk quotes them with their honest unit: a closed refrigerator holds food on the safe side of cold for around four hours through an outage, a half-full freezer for about a day, a packed freezer near two - the unit being a morning's market run and a weekend, not a number to gamble past. The temperature doing the maths is four degrees: above it the bacterial clock starts, and the food's own temperature is the meter, which is why the whole strategy is one sentence - keep the door as the one asset that cannot be bought back after it is spent.</p>
<h2>The habits that stretch the hours</h2>
<ul><li><b>One open, one list:</b> write what the next meal needs while the power is still a rumor you hope against; every extra door-open trades minutes of cold for curiosity. A torch aimed at the fridge shelf is the entire product this habit needs.</li>
<li><b>Jugs of water do the heavy lifting:</b> water holds cold better than air; keep filled jugs on the fridge shelves and in the freezer's empty corners - frozen bottles become both thermal mass and the next day's cold water, and a packed freezer outruns an expensive one with one carton in it.</li>
<li><b>Cluster the frozen block:</b> grouped food shares its cold mass and thaws slower than the same food scattered - move the meat, fish and ready-food into one corner of the freezer compartment, together, the way a market woman keeps ice among the fish and not beside it.</li>
<li><b>Cool packs ride on top:</b> cold sinks; frozen bottles or packs on the top shelf bank a real few degrees across the whole cabinet - the freezer's own trickle to the fridge below becomes this page's upgrade.</li></ul>
<h2>When power returns: the decision list, not the nose</h2>
<p>Smell is a test for spoilage organisms, not the pathogenic ones, which are odourless, tasteless and entirely capable of being the reason a household spends two nights on the toilet. So the four-hour line decides, and the desk's list is blunt: leftovers, cooked food, dairy, cut fruit and soft cheeses that rode out a warm fridge go in the bin without ceremony; raw meat and poultry that lost its chill for hours go too - the money saved is a bet with a named price. The freezer's mercy clause is published and real: food that still holds ice crystals, or reads as fridge-cold, can be cooked at once or refrozen without gamble - crystals or four degrees is the whole verdict, and it is the agencies' own rule.</p>
<h2>The generator arithmetic this page is really for</h2>
<p>If your outages come with a plan - a gen run twice a day, an inverter with the fridge on it - the strategy changes only in confidence: brief, scheduled power is the long outage that never happens, so the fridge goes on the circuit worth its fuel before the lights do (the load-maths already on this desk's power pages), and the door discipline still saves the fuel. If there is no plan, the closed-door budget is the plan, and this page's list is what to eat before hour four and what to surrender after it.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Four hours is the fridge's honest word; a packed freezer doubles and doubles it again; ice crystals are mercy, a warm kitchen counter is not; and the sniff test is banned from this household's protocol by this sentence - the nose has never once detected the germ that matters, and the bin is the cheapest insurance the outage ever sold.</p>"""),

 ("cockroach-fridge-motor-bay",
  "FIX",
  "The roaches are not in your cupboard, they are in the fridge's engine: the bay-first fix",
  "Why the warm motor compartment is the nest, why the spray can is the wrong tool twice over, and the gel-and-exclusion sequence that actually ends it.",
  """<p>Kitchens chasing roaches scrub cupboards and bait shelves for weeks while the colony sleeps twenty feet away from the food, in the one warm, oily, undisturbed compartment in the house: the machine room behind your fridge. The compressor bay is to a Lagos kitchen what a gutter pit is to a compound - the harbourage every spray misses because it is never the surface that was treated. Fix the bay and the cupboard problems stop appearing; treat the cupboard and the bay repopulates it by Thursday.</p>
<h2>The two sentences of entomology this fix needs</h2>
<p>Cockroaches want four things: warmth, food, water and a tight edge to press their bodies against - and the fridge's rear bay serves all four: the compressor's steady heat, motor-oil film and dust on the fins, crumbs and spills under the unit, and a thousand ideal seams. The second fact is why they ride the broadcast: German roaches in particular move along the warmth and the wall voids the fridge shares with the sockets and cabinets, which is why the animals you switch on light in one kitchen at midnight came - and go - through the appliance's own compartments. The bay is the hub; every other sighting is a branch.</p>
<h2>The sequence, in order, because order is the treatment</h2>
<ol><li><b>Pull and unplug:</b> the job starts at the socket, not the spray - the whole fix happens on a pulled appliance. Roll it out, check the rear for the tell-tale (speckled droppings, egg cases the size of a kidney bean, the oily smell that is their congregation, not the motor's).</li>
<li><b>Vacuum first, deep:</b> the creviced bay, the drip tray, the base pan under the compressor - suction takes the population, the eggs and the droppings that mark the trail; dispose of the bag outside, immediately, or you have relocated the colony into the bin and the bin into the kitchen.</li>
<li><b>Brush the condenser while you are there:</b> the dust coat on the fins is both their carpet and the reason the fridge runs hot and hungry - one dry brush ends two bills (this desk's cooling-coils guide covers the energy half).</li>
<li><b>Gel bait, not fog:</b> pea-sized dots in every corner of the bay's structure, behind the kick strip and where the line runs - bait is the colony's own courier service, the poisoned return that reaches the eggs' hatchlings; sprays kill what they touch and scatter the rest into the wall voids you just failed to treat.</li>
<li><b>A thin dust line where they walk:</b> food-grade diatomaceous earth or borax in a barely-visible veil behind the unit and along the baseboard - the pile is the failure mode: an inch of fluff, and they walk around it like a hedge. This is the whole technique: a road dust looks like and cannot cross.</li>
<li><b>Seal and starve:</b> the gap between the floor and the wall's skirt, the pipe collars at the back, the open cupboard void beside the motor - a little expanding foam or cement after the bait has finished its week. No cardboard boxes on or behind the fridge: glue and corrugation are the species' favourite apartment; everything stored there moves into lidded plastic.</li></ol>
<h2>The safety boundaries, both ends</h2>
<p>Against the machine: aerosol anywhere near a running motor is how a kitchen gets a second incident - solvent mist plus electrical sparks is a fire the can's own warning prints for the same reason this page quotes it, and a wet-washed bay short-cuts the appliance that shelters it. Against the food: roaches cross from the bay into the cabinet through the shared seams - after the treatment week, the interior gets its honest audit (all open packs into jars, gaskets wiped, shelves washed), because the bay fix protects the kitchen only if the kitchen was not the second nest. And the household-level truth: in a block of flats, your gel cannot win a building's war - widespread or neighbouring sightings make it a landlord-and-professional conversation, and this desk draws the line there without embarrassment.</p>
<h2>The verdict, as a rule</h2>
<p>Pull it out, vacuum the bay, bait the structure, dust the roads, seal the doors, and bin the cardboard - once, properly, in that order, beats every spray you have already bought. The fridge's engine room will be warm again tomorrow; make sure only one species finds that comfortable.</p>"""),

 ("doors-sticking-rainy-season",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "Doors that stick in the rains: hinges before the planer, planer before the prayer",
  "Which way wood actually moves, the two-screw hinge fix that closes half of all sticky doors, and the strike-plate check to do before you cut anything.",
  """<p>The door that swings shut in December and shoulders its frame open by the third rain of June is not failing - it is working exactly as wood does, swelling across the grain as the air loads it with water, the reveal you barely measured closing to nothing. Every sticky-door fix on the internet starts with a plane for the same reason most of them end badly: the wood is rarely the real misfit, the hanging is. The order below costs nothing to try and everything to skip; do it in sequence and the saw stays in the shed.</p>
<h2>Read the door before you touch it</h2>
<ul><li><b>Where it rubs tells you why:</b> mark the edge with a pencil swipe and swing - a bind along the top corner on the handle side means the door has dropped on its hinges; a uniform bind on the whole latch edge means swelling across the width; a bind at the bottom that clears when someone lifts the handle is a door hung too low, again a hinge story, not a wood story.</li>
<li><b>The lift test:</b> slide a wedge or a coin under the bottom and lift while it swings - the sticky door that closes like a dream while lifted is hanging in air, and its fix is screws before sawdust.</li>
<li><b>The season test:</b> same door, dry week. If it swings free, the house is breathing with the weather, and a cut made now becomes a rattle in January - the harmattan shrinkage this desk's wood guide covers from the other side.</li></ul>
<h2>The two-screw fix, which is the whole trade secret</h2>
<p>Most sag starts at the top hinge, where the whole door's weight levers the two screws nearest the door out of their jamb holes, and the frame's fixing screws then bite only the soft jamb timber or - in many Nigerian doors - into a hollow block through an oversized hole. The classic repair is honest mechanics: replace the two long screws of the top hinge - the ones pointing into the jamb - with screws long enough to pass through the jamb and grip the frame behind it, pulling the whole top of the door back onto its hinge line. A metal frame with a block behind it needs the anchor's answer - strip, fresh plugs or a chemically set fixing - and that is a drill-and-decide afternoon, not a woodwork job.</p>
<h2>The hardware that is not the wood</h2>
<p>Only the latch sticks and the rest swings free? The door has not grown; the frame has moved or the plate has crept. A strike plate carries the door's closing blow for years - tighten its screws (they pull loose and then the latch drops into air), shift it a millimetre or two with a chisel's honest nudge, deepen the pocket a shave with the same chisel, and the latch catches like the door was regreased, because for the price of a screwdriver it was.</p>
<h2>When it truly is the wood - cut it like a joiner</h2>
<ol><li>Take the door off; a planed edge in place plans the floor too. Find the bind by pencil lines already on it, mark the high face's full length.</li>
<li>Remove less than you want - a millimetre, a pass, re-hang, re-test in the same wet weather that complained; swelling is across the width, so a little off the binding edge can return the full reveal.</li>
<li><b>Seal every face and both long edges.</b> The edge is the door's straw: an unprimed, unpainted edge wicks rain-season water straight back into the wood and the door re-swells within a week, mid-passing the finish you just cut away. Prime, then paint, both edges and a foot onto both faces - the six-inch seal is why joiners' doors stay quiet and DIY-cut doors come back fighting.</li>
<li>Wax the closing edges - candle, paraffin-free soap, the door's own new friction budget; and for a door welded shut by years of painted edges, run a sharp knife around the seam before any shoulder - forcing a stuck painted door is how paint cracks all the way down the stile, the peeling the bathroom-paint guide then has to argue with.</li></ol>
<p>One material exception, printed so nobody planes a steel door in the rain: metal frames and doors do not swell - their stick, if there is one, is movement, rust or a hinge under strain, and the fix list above stops at the screws. If it is still sticky: the house is moving around the door, and that reading belongs to this desk's crack guides, not this page.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Lift before you plane, screws before sawdust, and seal everything you cut within the same afternoon the rains gave you the problem: a swollen door is the house's weather report, and the correct reply keeps the door on its hinges, the frame in its square, and the planer - like the argument - as the last resort you will not need.</p>"""),

 ("tv-mount-on-block-wall",
  "FIX",
  "Mounting a TV on a block wall: the anchors that hold when the plaster refuses",
  "Plaster is decoration; the block is structure - sleeve versus toggle, hollow versus sandcrete, the tile-drilling laws, and the one thing to trace before the drill bites.",
  """<p>A television mount is a promise from four fixings: the full weight of screen and bracket hanging off four pencil-thin points of steel. In a Nigerian house, what those points reach depends on exactly one fact - what is behind the plaster - and the entire craft of the mount is knowing the answer before buying the bracket, because "the wall is fine" is the sentence that ends walls. This page is the difference between an anchor that is bolted into structure and one that is merely bolted.</p>
<h2>Know your wall's own anatomy</h2>
<p>Most living-room walls are sandcrete or concrete block with a skin of render, and the render - the smooth painted face - carries nothing ever; forty millimetres of plaster is decoration. So the first test is the knock-and-look: a solid block wall rings tight and grinds the masonry bit through its full thickness; a hollow clay or concrete block gives a drum and, worse, a web of cavities where a rawlplug will spin into air or crumble its own hole wider. Above doors and windows runs the lintel; at floor level, a course of solid. The mount's honest friends, in order: solid block, a solid course, a timber batten frame fixed to solid, or a stand. The wall skin of a hollow block is not on that list, and the bracket box will not warn you - the warning is this paragraph.</p>
<h2>The hardware, by the wall's answer</h2>
<ul><li><b>Solid block:</b> sleeve or wedge anchors, or the bracket maker's own fixings where the instruction says them - matched to the drill size the pack prints (a bigger hole is a loose anchor, and loose is how TVs fall at night), set deeper than the bolt's length and driven with the sleeve fully buried; a chemical-resin stud is the patient answer for anything heavy at the frame's worst edge.</li>
<li><b>Hollow block:</b> toggle wings grip the back of the skin - fine for a shelf, not a desk's recommendation for sixty inches of lever arm. The correct fix is spreading the load across the structure: a plywood or timber backing bonded and screwed across several webs to reach them, or drilling through a solid course; where the wall truly cannot carry it, the professional core-fills and ties a plate to the blockwork behind the mount - a pro's day, not a Saturday's.</li>
<li><b>The floor's own cheat:</b> the cabinet or stand with the strap to the wall is the landlord's answer, the rented flat's answer, and sometimes the only answer a block this old deserves. Nobody who has seen the floor's face remembers a stand as a compromise.</li></ul>
<h2>Before the first hole: the two traces</h2>
<p>Walls carry the house's nerves and no builder ever drew where - so the desk's rule before the drill: find the sockets and switches in a line, and assume their cable runs straight up or down from them like an arrow; mount centres above a socket or beside a spur are the two holes you do not drill, and the spur's hidden drop behind a plastered channel is the third. Same for the TV's own plan: a cable dropped inside the wall looks finished and becomes next year's live-wire mystery - the surface trunking is the honest cover, cheap and cuttable, and it keeps the next person's drill safe. If the drill touches anything with current or water in it, stop, call the professional and let them open it properly: a patched wall is cheaper than the two versions of that sentence that end in insurance.</p>
<h2>Tiles, the special warning</h2>
<p>The TV wall that is tiled changes two laws of drilling, not the wall's weakness. Hammer action on a tile face is a hammer to its glaze: start with a plain carbide bit, no percussion, slow and cool (the bit's own rest in water is its whole lifespan), tape the spot against skating, and keep the drill in rotation until you are through the glaze and into the block behind - then hammer. Tile's other betrayal is its edge: the hole that starts a centimetre off the grout line can run the crack to the corner of every tile in the room. Measure to the grout, not to the pattern, and cut your layout from the tile joints, because the tiles were laid straight on a wall that mostly was not.</p>
<h2>Hang like a professional and test like one</h2>
<ol><li>Level the plate, mark, re-level after the first anchor - the tightening of every anchor walks the plate a fraction and the level catches it before the wall argues.</li>
<li>Two people for two reasons: the weight and the sight lines - one holds the screen while the other checks it is at the height the sofa's neck chose, not the box's optimism.</li>
<li>The security hardware is not optional: the anti-tip strap, the bracket's locking screw or the safety band, all exist for the household that dances, the child that climbs, the generator that thumps at 2am - install them even on a wall you trust.</li>
<li>The pull test, once cabling and the box are off: a year's worth of shoulder into the screen's bottom corner, watching the anchors move nothing and listening to nothing grind, before you stand it on and walk away proud.</li></ol>
<h2>The desk's verdict</h2>
<p>Solid block, sleeve anchors, correct bit, traced wall, strap on: a good hang that outlives the television. Hollow block, plaster alone, the "plastic wall plug in the corner of the bracket": an argument with gravity that is already lost - and gravity does not negotiate mid-party.</p>"""),

 ("burning-plastic-smell-socket",
  "FIX",
  "The burning-plastic smell from a socket: five minutes of discipline, then a professional",
  "Why 'it still works' is the most dangerous sentence in the house, the sniff map that saves the electrician time, and what nobody should open.",
  """<p>A socket smells of hot plastic and the appliance still runs - this is the moment the house's wiring asks for help in the only language it has, and the two worst possible responses are pretending not to notice and opening the wall to 'just check'. The smell has a short list of causes, all of them electrical, most of them a bad connection quietly cooking insulation; the good news is that a household can halve the danger in five minutes with nothing but a switchboard walk and the discipline to leave one circuit alone until the professional arrives.</p>
<h2>Why the smell means 'now', not 'eventually'</h2>
<p>Electricity heats what resists it, and in a wall the usual culprit is a connection that loosened - a terminal screw backed off over years of thermal cycling, a strand of undersized wire clamped under a terminal that wants more, or a plug whose pins have lost their spring in a socket that has seen a decade of heaters. Resistance at that one joint makes the joint hot; hot makes melted insulation; the plastic smell is the first finished product of the chain, and the second is arcing, which does not need to touch anything to ignite what drips below it. Nothing on that list announces itself with a spark first - which is why this desk prints the blunt line: a socket that smells is a socket out of service the same hour, whatever it costs the household's convenience.</p>
<h2>The five-minute sequence, in order</h2>
<ol><li><b>Unplug everything on the affected socket</b> - and look at what you unplugged: a plug with browned pins, a warped plastic skirt or a warm-to-hold brick is the fault already named, and the appliance's cable goes to a technician before it goes back in any wall.</li>
<li><b>Kill the circuit and leave it dead:</b> at the board, one switch off is the whole fix you are allowed to do yourself; the smell is not a reason to re-energise 'to see if it still does it' - the seeing is the expensive part.</li>
<li><b>The sniff map:</b> with the circuit off, walk the room and the next room and mark which outlets, spurs and the light fittings smell, where the wall is warmer than its neighbours to the back of the hand, whether any socket face is discoloured or bulging at the paint line. Two minutes with a pencil on paper, and the electrician arrives with the diagnosis half-read - which is what turns a probe-and-pray day into an hour of real repair.</li>
<li><b>Check what shares the suspected socket:</b> one spur feeding five outlets carries the same current through every loose joint it touches; the smell at one face can be the burn at another.</li></ol>
<h2>The boundary, stated without apology</h2>
<p>Inside the wall is the professional's house: no socket plate off, no terminal retightened by a household hand, no 'my cousin is good with electrics' - a meter, a torque and a supply-isolation practice are the tools that keep the cousin out of the accident report. The desk draws the line exactly at the plate. The same line, one floor up: if the smell meets a breaker that trips repeatedly on the same circuit, that is the protection doing its job at the last gate; the fix is the cause, never a heavier breaker - oversizing protection to stop the nuisance trip is how houses meet the fire instead of the warning.</p>
<h2>After the repair: what the electrician should find</h2>
<p>A competent diagnosis in an older house usually ends in one of a short list - a re-terminated joint, a replaced socket whose contacts have relaxed, a cable run that has been asked to carry more than its size (the kettle-heater-spaceheater trinity) and is upgraded or split, or a spur taken off the loop it never belonged on. The visit is also the free hour to do the walk the wiring-red-flags guide covers - scorched marks, daisy-chained adapters, the junction box that is just a twist of tape - so the same call closes the file and prevents its sequel.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Smell means unplug, off and call - the same hour, in that order, on a circuit you leave alone. A house that catches the smell is a house that got a warning; the warning's only job is to be taken seriously before the sequel arrives without the courtesy of a smell.</p>"""),

 ("water-tank-annual-clean",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The tank is the reason the kettle tastes fine: the annual scrub, done in the right order",
  "How often the roof-tank truthfully needs opening, the lid job that matters more than the scrub, and the chlorine maths the shop never explains.",
  """<p>Every stored-water complaint the kitchen notices - the flat taste, the silt that meets the pump when pressure dips, the film that skates across the bucket - was living in the tank first, quietly building for months. Roof tanks are not set-and-forget plumbing; they are open vessels that turn borehole or mains water into a held inventory, and held water asks three questions every year: what settled, what grew, and what got in. This page is the honest maintenance calendar, from the lid to the last rinse, with the two numbers the treatment plan runs on.</p>
<h2>The real schedule, set by the source not the calendar</h2>
<p>The desk's working line: every household with a tank opens it for inspection once a year, and the ones that need the full clean twice a year are identifiable from the lid alone - a borehole that carries silt (the orange stain of iron, the fine sand that never fully settles out), a roof-fed or 'make-up' line drawing from a questionable source, or a tank whose vent mesh is last season's engineering. Mains-water-only houses in good areas are the lucky exception that can go longer between scrubs and never between inspections. The sign that the schedule missed: after a pump shutdown, the first draw is cloudy or gritty - that is the settled bed being stirred, and the tank is overdue, full stop.</p>
<h2>The clean, in the order the water cooperates</h2>
<ol><li><b>Plan the drain:</b> a tank full of dirty water needs somewhere honest to go - the garden line, not the kitchen taps' own suction. Close the outlet, run the level down or pump it out to what the scrub needs.</li>
<li><b>Enter right or don't enter:</b> the desk prints the safety line plainly - a sealed tank is an confined space: no ladder-in-and-hold-your-breath descents, no candles to 'test the air'; a scrub from the hatch with a long-handled brush reaches the silt that matters, and a real internal scrub of a big tank is a paid job with someone holding the ladder and the lid open. Chlorine fumes and a closed roof in the sun are the reason this paragraph exists.</li>
<li><b>Scrub, don't just rinse:</b> the biofilm clings - the walls' slick and the lid's underside are the two surfaces the bucket-and-hose routine skips and the brush never should. No detergents, no 'tank cleaner' chemicals: the water you will drink is the only test that counts, and soap is the one thing the tank must never taste.</li>
<li><b>Drain, rinse, refill, and the first draw goes to the garden</b> - not the kettle - until the water runs clear and the smell is just water.</li></ol>
<h2>Chlorine: the one number, and why the shop's advice is wrong</h2>
<p>Shock treatment is dosing known-strength chlorine into a known volume and letting contact time do the work - the honest version of the household's 'some bleach' habit is that it is a measurement, not a pour: the tank's litres are maths from its diameter and depth, the product label states its percentage, and the safe drinking-water target the public-health guidance repeats is a small, measurable residual after the contact time - which means too little is theatre and too much is a chemical tasting party with your kettle as the judge. The desk's practical route for a household: use the local water authority's or a public-health leaflet's dosing table for your exact product and volume, keep a copy with the chlorine, and after a shock, the tank refills with fresh and the first draws are tested before the drinking line reopens. And the truth the sellers omit: chlorine kills what swims, does nothing about the silt bed or the iron - a tank that needs scrubbing every year may need the borehole's sediment stage checked (the water-treatment guide names the filters that belong to that fight).</p>
<h2>The lid: half the annual job, ninety percent of the prevention</h2>
<p>Everything the scrub removes gets in through a gap: the mesh on the vents that mosquitoes and leaf-fall shred, the lid seal that a brick 'to keep it closed' has warped, the overflow pipe that was cut short and open when it should wear a meshed bend, the float valve whose seat ages and drips the level down into an air gap nothing was designed for. The annual inspection's checklist is the lid's: closed flush, screened at every opening, no light inside (light grows the green), and the tank's own stand clear of the ground the pump splash soaks. A tank that is sealed and dark needs a fraction of the scrubbing of one that is not - the free half of this page.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Inspect every year at the start of the rains, scrub when the floor of the tank says so, dose chlorine as a measurement from a label and not a mood, and treat the lid like the tank's front door - because every contaminant this guide lists has a lid story, and every clean tank this desk has opened with a happy owner had one thing in common: someone who checked the lid in March.</p>"""),

 ("toilet-clog-how-to-plunge",
  "FIX",
  "The toilet that will not take: the plunger technique that works, and the two habits that end the call",
  "Why the sink plunger fails, the flange and the seal that are the entire technique, and the point where 'keep pouring hot water' becomes a cracked bowl.",
  """<p>A blocked toilet is the one plumbing emergency every household believes it can handle with improvised tools, and the reason the improvisation fails is that the tool is half the technique: the right plunger, with a seal, works by pushing and pulling water - not air - through the blockage, and the bad version of this day ends with a full bowl, a exhausted afternoon and a plumber's bill for a job the right ten minutes would have closed. The whole skill is in the seal and the pull; the rest is knowing when to stop.</p>
<h2>The tool, because the wrong one dooms the session</h2>
<p>The cup plunger - the flat rubber disc for sinks - cannot seal a toilet's curved outlet, and a toilet bought with the wrong plunger is an afternoon of splashing. The toilet plunger is the flange type: the cup with the soft ring that extends from its bottom to fit inside the bowl's hole. The desk adds the tool nobody buys until too late - a pair of rubber gloves, an old towel, and a bucket for the water you bail out of a full bowl so the plunge has room to work; the plunge fails physically when the bowl is to the rim, and bailing is not giving up, it is setting up.</p>
<h2>The technique, which is mostly the pull</h2>
<ol><li><b>Protect the floor, close the valve</b> (the little tap behind the pan), lift the seat - and if the water is high, bail it to half with the cup from the kitchen, the towel under the rim.</li>
<li><b>Seat the flange inside the hole, not on it:</b> the seal is everything; press in gently until the rubber hugs the ceramic and the trapped air pockets are what the first soft stroke, not a heroic one, sends away - the air compresses and does nothing; water does not compress, and the force that moves the block is a column of water.</li>
<li><b>Plunge as a rhythm, not a fight:</b> firm pushes and - the part nobody does - strong pulls, ten to fifteen cycles with the seal unbroken, the handle staying vertical; the pull is what loosens and draws the mass back from the grip the trap has on it. One long unbroken session is less effective than three of these with a pause to watch what drained.</li>
<li><b>Test with a half-bucket flush, never the full handle:</b> when the level drops the way it used to, the job is done, and the full flush on the end-of-session nerves is how a half-cleared clog becomes the full one, twice.</li></ol>
<h2>The things the internet owes a correction about</h2>
<p><b>Hot water and detergent:</b> the gentle version - a pot of hot (never boiling) water with a squeeze of soap, poured from waist height, left ten minutes - genuinely softens organic masses and costs nothing; the boiling version is how a ceramic bowl that has met cold morning air cracks along its foot, and a cracked pan is the whole plumber's bill for a Tuesday's clog. <b>Chemical openers:</b> the caustic liquids work slowly on some blockages and their speed on porcelain, seals and skin is a poor trade; worse, they turn the overflowing water of the failed attempt into the burn that teaches the next lesson expensively - the desk's order: plunger first, chemicals never the first call, and a house that pours them must tell the plumber, because the plumber's hands are above your decision. <b>The wire hanger trick:</b> a coat-hanger snake is a legitimate tool for the right trap - and the tool that scratches glaze, jams against the bend, or pushes a toy two inches further into the place from which it becomes the pan's replacement; if something solid went in the bowl, the honest next step is the pro who owns the proper auger.</p>
<h2>When plunging is the wrong tool</h2>
<p>The signs that this is not your clog: water rising in the shower or the yard drain when the toilet runs, gurgles in other traps (the block is downstream, in the soil line or the soakaway's entry - this house's whole drainage is one system and the toilet is its alarm), a full block that no plunge has moved in three sessions, or the recurring same-pipe blockage that says a root, a collapsed run or a belly in the line - each of those is a professional conversation, not a Saturday, and the soakaway guide's section on what the field tells you belongs next to this paragraph in the same binder. The one pre-call the household does control: stop running water into the system - the family's taps, the washing machine, 'just one bucket' - because every added litre finds the same blocked exit and starts at the nearest open trap, which is the floor of whichever room you least want flooding.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Bail, seal, ten firm cycles with real pulls, half-bucket test - and three sessions is the ceiling before the call, the same ceiling after which the hanger, the chemicals and the boiling pot stop being folk wisdom and start being deposits on the repair bill. The prevention half fits in one line: the pan's two jobs are paper and the things it was made for; everything else - the wipes' 'flushable' promise included - is a future visit to the drain that never forgave them.</p>"""),

 ("generator-rainy-season-safety",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The generator in the rain: the six rules that keep the blackout out of the accident report",
  "Water and electricity do negotiate, and badly - the positioning maths, the cable law, the bonding paragraph the installers skip, and the CO rule that does not pause for the storm.",
  """<p>The rains change the generator's risk profile completely while the household's habits stay identical: the same machine, the same cables, but now on wet ground, under nothing, with damp fuel and connections that were tolerated in March. Every rule on this page exists because one failure mode in a wet-weather gen setup ends in a dead person, a burned compound or an appliance graveyard - and the honest part is that the whole protection package costs a plan, a length of good cable and a habit or two.</p>
<h2>Position: the three walls and none of them wrong</h2>
<p>A generator in the rains wants exactly what it wants in harmattan - outdoors, level, off the ground water reaches - and one thing more: a roof that is not a room. The open-sided canopy (or the wall-and-tarp arrangement with the sides the wind cannot close) keeps the rain off the alternator and the fuel cap while leaving the exhaust and the heat nowhere to go but out and up; a generator inside a closed porch, a half-wall corner with no cross-flow, or 'just for tonight' by the door is where this page's first body count lives: exhaust goes where the air goes, and the house finds it by morning. The carbon-monoxide rule that does not take a rainy-season holiday: fumes travel with wind through open windows, under gates, up through floor gaps - the machine's face points away from every opening, and nobody in the house sleeps between the openings. Ground, too: blocks under the feet, because the compound's puddle line rises where the machine sits in April and not in January, and a gen sitting in water is a gen whose frame is now live with the whole yard watching.</p>
<h2>Cables, the law of them</h2>
<p>Three facts and a rule. The extension cords that live behind the house are thin, often spliced, and rated for nothing that matters; indoor flex in wet service heats along its whole length and the 'warm cable' is the warning shot of its insulation's retirement. Water finds the connections: the socket you plug into in the rain is two pieces of exposed metal held near each other by hope - every joint off the ground, every plug in a drip loop (the cable dips below the socket and the water runs off the low point instead of into it), the connections under the canopy and not beside it. The daisy-chain of adapters is a parallel-circuit fire in a costume. And the rule that ends all three paragraphs: a generator that feeds the house through a socket - the plug-into-the-wall reverse feed, the 'car cable with two plugs' - kills the line worker or the neighbour on the same transformer the day the mains come back, is illegal in every jurisdiction that has words for it, and is the reason this desk prints, twice: the safe house connection is a changeover switch, installed and bonded, which means the generator connects through the switch and the switch says which source the house may have - there is no clever version of doing it with a plug.</p>
<h2>Fuel, starting and the wet-engine day</h2>
<p>Water in fuel is the rains' own generator killer: the cap's seal ages, the jerry can lives outside, a cup of condensation in a tank that runs rich ends as a no-start and a sump-full of patience, so the fuel can lives capped, upright and dry, and a machine that has sat gets its drain bowl opened before the pull. The plug and the coil's friends - the spark-plug boot, the air filter, the pull-cord hand - all hate damp, and the honest routine is a dry run at the start of the season, the carburettor drained if the weeks look empty, and the old stabiliser question answered the way small engines forgive: fresh fuel, not stored fuel. The earthling paragraph the installers skip: a standalone gen whose body is not earthed to a proper rod is a machine that decides which of its parts becomes the path of least resistance when something inside it sheds an insulation - the rod and the bonding are one afternoon with the right tradesman and the whole reason this desk can write 'if a bare frame ever tells your feet a story, the bonding is missing' as a diagnostic and not a rumour.</p>
<h2>The switchboard of small habits</h2>
<ul><li>Hands dry, boots on, one person at the gen while the other stays clear of the house cables when plugging in - the pair system is how the compound avoids the second victim.</li>
<li>Everything off before the plug: the gen starts into a dead house and carries loads in ones after, the biggest motor (the fridge, the pump) last - the surge law from the inverter page applies here with more noise.</li>
<li>The rain stops, the gen keeps running - and its cables are still wet; the pack-down is a dry-morning job, coiled off the ground, wound with the connections away from the puddle it just survived.</li>
<li>Children, the tarp, the hot muffler: the three objects that belong in the same sentence every season the gen gets repositioned.</li></ul>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Canopied, chocked, bonded, through a real changeover, with cables that look like they were chosen and not inherited - the compound that runs the same generator the same year the rains do is the one whose blackout story stays a story about the lights. The other version of this season's list of items is short, and this page exists so the household reads it before the rain, not after.</p>"""),

 ("cooking-oil-fire-plan",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The pan that caught: the oil-fire plan, written for the way Lagos kitchens actually fight it",
  "Why water is the wrong answer with a physics lesson attached, the lid and the cloth that are the right ones, and the two moves that turn a bad evening into a hospital story.",
  """<p>Oil fires are the kitchen's one emergency where the instinctive response - grab water, throw it - is the accelerant, and where the correct responses look, to a panicked household, like doing nothing. The whole plan below is three sentences of physics and a sequence of six moves; the reason it works is that a fat fire is not a bigger fire, it is a different fire, with its own rules, and the rules are knowable before the oil gets to its smoke and someone's reflex beats the training.</p>
<h2>Why water turns a pan into a torch</h2>
<p>Burning oil sits far above water's boiling point; poured on, the water sinks through the fat - it is heavier - flashes to steam in an instant, and the expanding steam throws a spray of burning oil upward and outward, igniting all of it at once. That is the entire mechanism of the flash that takes a pan fire to a ceiling fire in the space of a shout, and it is also why 'a little water to cool it' is not half a remedy; it is a full detonator. The second physics fact the plan leans on: fire needs the vapour above the oil, not the oil itself - smother the vapour and the fire is a memory in seconds.</p>
<h2>The six moves, in order</h2>
<ol><li><b>Off:</b> the burner's knob is the fire's tap; a pan on a dead flame is a candle in a pan, and everything after this is easier. If the pan can slide off the stove to the clear tile - with an oven glove, toward you, on the stovetop's own tray - do that; a small pan fire moved is a fire half won.</li>
<li><b>Lid:</b> the flat, metal lid slides on from the far side, pushing the blanket of air ahead of it rather than stirring it - and the pan that is covered stays covered. This is the whole remedy: no lid of the pan's family exists in most Nigerian kitchens, which is why the plan's shopping list starts here.</li>
<li><b>The damp cloth, if there is no lid:</b> a real soaking, then wrung to merely wet, laid across the whole top like a blanket - the same smothering, for one or two minutes less gracefully. The dry tea-towel is fuel; the damp one is a lid made of textile; this paragraph is exactly the line between the two sentences.</li>
<li><b>Nothing that breathes:</b> no fan to cool it, no 'carry it outside' (the walk fans it and the curtains are along the route), no throwing flour, baking powder or salt at it in the hope of powder-magic - a cloud of fine powder is its own accelerant, and the bag that feeds a pan fire the way the jug of water does.</li>
<li><b>Bicarbonate, only for the small and just-born:</b> a generous fistful of baking soda smothers by releasing its own carbon dioxide when it hits heat - the real version of the folk trick - but its power is measured in pans, not in the fire that has already taken the extractor: a flame taller than the pan has passed the powder stage, and everything after this line changes.</li>
<li><b>The fire blanket or the exit:</b> a small wall-mounted fire blanket bought this month (the class rated for kitchens answers 'what do I do the second time, faster') ends a pan in one wrapped pull; anything past the pan's lip - the oil flame that has gone up the cabinet, the extractor alight - is the house's two moves: everyone out, then the call, then the fighting, if it is ever fought, from outside with the door to the room shut behind you.</li></ol>
<h2>The prevention half, because the plan's best scene never happens</h2>
<p>Oil heats faster than a distracted person thinks - the pan that is 'just warming' is a pan on the desk's watch list, and the fry that goes unwatched for a phone call is how the evening's headline gets written. The habits that are worth the boring: the handle turned in (the elbow that snags the cloth is the other classic path to a lap full of fire), no water near the hot oil in either direction (the wet pan, the frozen thing dunked into it - the same steam physics working in reverse, and it spatters the burn and the flame both), the gas off at the cylinder when the cooking is done (the safety page's own rule), and the pan's lid kept on the stove's shelf where the dark at 11pm can find it by feel, because the first four seconds of the fire are the whole battle and the household's reflexes are shopping for it in advance.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Off, lid, leave it covered - the fire's obituary is three sentences. Water and powder at anything taller than the pan are the moves the video of the second fire shows, and the second fire is the one with the burns: the kitchen that owns a lid and knows the sequence never writes that script.</p>"""),

 ("wall-cracks-when-serious",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The crack in the wall: the two-minute read that says paint job or structural engineer",
  "Hairline and honest against stepped and widening, the tape test that turns 'maybe' into data, and the four signs the wall's story is about the ground.",
  """<p>Almost every crack is cosmetic and almost every household still pays the structural anxiety in full, because nobody hands them the reading key. The honest frame first: a house is a rigid thing on soft ground with seasons that move it, and a wall that never showed one hairline in its life is usually a wall too young to speak about. The useful question is never 'is there a crack' - it is 'what shape, which direction is it growing, and what does the rest of the house agree', and this page is the two-minute version of asking exactly those three.</p>
<h2>The two families, named by their shape</h2>
<ul><li><b>The plaster's own cracks - fine, webbed, shallow:</b> hairlines that follow no line you could draw with a ruler, craze the width of a fingernail's edge, the map that paint sits on and light rakes along. Causes are the render's own life - cement that shrank as it cured, a skim too thick, the harmattan opening the fine crazing and the rains closing it. These are cosmetic with a capital and their only structural sin is being a channel for water into a skin that was thin to begin with; they get filled, taped where two materials meet, and painted.</li>
<li><b>The wall's cracks - straight-ish, continuous, deeper than the skin:</b> the diagonal at a door's corner, the stair-step that walks the block courses, the horizontal that runs along the mortar line, the vertical that separates one wall from another exactly at the joint where two builds met. These are geometry speaking: movement of the thing behind the plaster, and their reading needs the page's second half.</li></ul>
<h2>The two-minute read, in the order that produces data</h2>
<p>First the light: a torch flat against the wall at dusk turns every shadow-line honest - a crack in the paint only will vanish under a fresh skim's shadow while the real one keeps its black edge. Then the tape test, which is the whole diagnostic in one strip: a piece of cheap masking tape across the crack's middle, dated in pencil, photographed, checked monthly - a crack that is finished has a tape that stays straight across it, and a crack that is still moving shreds, bows or opens past the tape's edge, which is the exact sentence the engineer wants from you, with your dates on it. Then the width test, the one that sounds trivial and decides consultations: a coin in the gap - a crack that takes a thin coin by its rim is telling the house's story, not the paint's. Then the company the crack keeps, which is the paragraph that turns a single crack into a diagnosis: the door in the same room that started sticking the year the crack appeared (the doors page's 'the house is moving' answer belongs here, not there), the window that no longer slides the way its siblings do, the floor with the new level to it, the external plinth where the ground and the wall's foot now disagree - a crack with a door's testimony is a set of symptoms, and sets get the professional.</p>
<h2>The four signs the ground is in the story</h2>
<ol><li><b>Stepped and diagonal together on the same wall,</b> the pattern of a footing that dipped on one side: the block courses tell where the movement is, and the diagonal corner crack that crosses courses tells which way the house leaned.</li>
<li><b>The ground-floor version is worse than the upstairs one,</b> as a rule - the movement lives where the foundations live, and the crack that starts at the floor line and climbs is the same sentence in a taller font.</li>
<li><b>Seasonal opening that has begun to leave a permanent gap:</b> the house that closes its crack in the rains and opens it in the dry weeks has a breathing story; the year the gap stops closing is the year the ground's repair of its own movement ran out - and the tape with its dates is what proves which of the two you are watching.</li>
<li><b>The water accomplice:</b> the crack that appeared along the path of a leak, a blocked gutter's overflow line, or the soakaway field the plumbing page warned about (saturated ground beside a footing is the most preventable cause on this list - the gutters guide and the soakaway guide are this crack's two alibis, and both are fixable this month).</li></ol>
<h2>Who you call, with what you hand them</h2>
<p>The plasterer's job is the first family, the fill-and-tape-and-paint close of cosmetic crazing, and the honest quote begins with 'it's only the render' with a light in his hand. The structural engineer's job is the second family, and the house should meet them prepared: the dated photographs, the tape's history, the width by the coin, the list of doors and floors that agree - the professional's visit is halved and the recommendations get cheaper in direct proportion. The questions a Nigerian house should expect them to ask about - the soil's own character (the swelling clays of some estates are a named actor), the nearby digging (the neighbour's new foundation, the drain trench, the road works), the water's management (the same soakaway and gutters list) - are the case's facts, not the client's confessions; a movement story is read from all of them at once. The serious-quickly signs that skip both trades and go to the exits and the pro this season: a crack wide enough for the coin's full edge with a bulge at the wall's face, a window or door frame suddenly racked where its siblings are square, the ceiling that sagged in the same rooms (the plaster page's sagging sequence runs this floor), or the new crack with the new tilt - that group is the house's last calm sentence before shouting, and the tape test is for everything before it.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Light, tape, coin, company - the two-minute exam every crack deserves before money moves. Hairlines take a paint day; the deep shapes with witnesses take the engineer, and the one the household can fix from the ladder of prevention is the water: the gutters clear and the soakaway's thirst honoured before the first hairline darkened into a story.</p>"""),

 ("flat-roof-ponding-and-leaks",
  "FIX",
  "The water that stays on the roof an hour after rain is the water finding your ceiling",
  "Ponding as the diagnosis, not the weather: the slope maths in footsteps, the coatings that buy years not immunity, and the drain habits that cost nothing.",
  """<p>On a flat-roofed house, rain that leaves a sheet of water standing an hour after the sky cleared is not 'the roof being flat' - it is the roof telling you where it dipped. Concrete slabs are built with a nominal fall and a waterproof layer that ages, and every pond is a place where water sits long enough to interrogate every crack the summer baked. The fix list is short, the sequence matters, and the free part - keeping water moving - beats most of the paid part.</p>
<h2>Why standing water is the whole story</h2>
<p>Concrete is a sponge with good manners: water that drains away in ten minutes barely visits; water that sits for days enters. The pond also does the mechanical work - it freezes nothing in this climate, but it cycles heat and rain, opening the hairline along old joints, and it hosts the grit that holds moisture against the surface like a compress. The desk's plain rule for the inspection walk: the roof's future leaks are visible today as its puddles - mark the pond's outline with chalk or a line of cement on the day after rain, and the map is the work order.</p>
<h2>The slope, fixed in the order that lasts</h2>
<ol><li><b>Clear first:</b> parapet scuppers, roof drains and the gutters below them belong to this roof's drainage system, and the leaf-pack and mortar-crumb silt is why a designed fall becomes an accidental pond. Half the 'roof failure' calls this desk has heard end at the drain the builder blocked.</li>
<li><b>Read the fall:</b> a sound flat roof wants roughly a thumb's slope per metre toward its outlets - the practical version of that is the chalk-map and the bucket test: water poured at the high point should move within its minute; water that spreads in a circle has nowhere to go.</li>
<li><b>Re-slope where it dips:</b> the durable fix is levelling compound or a screed laid to the drains by the tradesman who owns the straightedge - a millimetre skim over a weak slab asks the slab to carry it, so the desk prints the boundary plainly: a new screed on a cracked or deflected slab is the professional's decision, not the Saturday's.</li>
<li><b>Then coat what still leaks through:</b> the bituminous paints and acrylic roof coatings are real products with honest lifespans - they buy years over sound concrete, not against a pond that remains. A coating over un-fixed ponding is wallpaper over a leak: better colour, same story. The manufacturer's own page states the product's lifespan and the surface prep it demands; prep is two-thirds of the job's longevity and all of its failure in the shortcut version.</li></ol>
<h2>The inside evidence, read in order</h2>
<p>The ceiling stain under a pond is the roof's receipt - the this-desk ceiling-stain page decodes the tide marks, and the 11pm-leak protocol tells you what to do during the storm itself; this page adds only the roof-side pair: when the stain maps to a chalk outline, the two documents become one case, and when the pond is above the bathroom rather than the bedroom, the water's two possible journeys (through the slab from above, along the pipe chase from within) get the honest before-and-after test the leak guide prints. And the danger that outranks the damp: water travels along the reinforcement and exits at the wall heads, so the crack at the ceiling's edge is a pond's signature, and the exposed steel staining rust through the soffit is the line at which the conversation stops being a roofer's and becomes a structural engineer's (the wall-cracks page's witness list applies indoors too).</p>
<h2>The calendar that costs nothing</h2>
<p>Twice a year the roof gets walked: before the rains - clears the drains, photographs the ponds from the last season (the chalk will have washed, the photos will not), tightens the parapet copings that drop their cracks into the system - and after harmattan, when the dust has packed every outlet like the roof's own silt trap. Between, the two free rules: nothing new sits on the roof without legs (the water must flow under the tank stand, and the slab's surface is not a storage yard), and the contractor who welds anything up there - the sign frame, the antenna mast, the new solar rail - is the author of next year's leak unless the penetration gets a proper collar and a written note on the roof's map. The pond is patient; the household cannot afford to be.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Mark the ponds, clear the paths, fix the fall before the coat, and walk the roof at both season turns - a flat roof is a drainage machine whose only failure mode is standing water, and standing water is the one part of the roof the household controls with a bucket, a brush and a photograph.</p>"""),

 ("pump-short-cycling-waterhammer",
  "FIX",
  "The pump that starts and stops every few seconds: the pressure tank's air charge and the valve",
  "Short-cycling named and explained, the tap-bang of waterhammer traced in one evening, and the two components that fail quietly long before the motor complains.",
  """<p>A pressure pump switching itself on for three seconds every twelve is not being busy - it is drowning in its own job, because the cushion that should let the system breathe has gone soft, and every dripping tap or half-seated flush is now a start command. Left alone, the cycling grinds the pump's life to months, bangs the pipes until joints loosen, and the desk's first sentence is the good news: the two usual causes are a ten-minute check and a part cheaper than the call-out, with one boundary - the pump head itself stays closed.</p>
<h2>The two symptoms, because they share a cause</h2>
<ul><li><b>Short-cycling:</b> the pressure system works by squeezing air in a small tank (vessel) while water runs into it; the air - compressible, patient - feeds the house between pump runs, and the switch tells the pump when the pressure band is done. When the air cushion is gone (the tank waterlogged, the bladder inside it burst) there is nothing between the pump and the tap: pressure snaps up, the switch cuts, a tap moves, pressure snaps down - the machine starts again, forever. The knock test the trade uses on the tank itself tells you which half is guilty: a vessel that sounds solid-full of water at every angle has lost its cushion.</li>
<li><b>Waterhammer:</b> the bang when a solenoid closes - the washing-machine valve, the flush - is the same missing cushion telling the pipes to ring like struck fencing; the air spring absorbs the stop, and with nothing to absorb it, the pressure wave throws the pipework. The banding the pipe straps and the loose joint it fatigues start as the noise and finish as the leak.</li></ul>
<h2>The evening's own sequence, up to the line</h2>
<ol><li><b>Find the ghost draw:</b> with everything off in the house, watch whether the pump still ticks on a cycle - a slow creep means a leak downstream (the cistern's silent flush, the garden tap, the ballcock that never quite seats; the tank-overflow guide's paragraph on the float is the same fix from the other end), and a no-traffic cycling with the lines still is the pump's side of the fault.</li>
<li><b>Isolate and test the vessel:</b> the power off, the system pressure bled to zero at an open tap, the tank's air valve checked with a bicycle-type gauge - if it reads nothing where the system wants a starting figure, the recharge is the air pump and five minutes of this page's whole household-accessible repertoire (the correct pre-charge number is printed on the pump's or vessel's own label; the desk publishes none because every model runs its own band). A valve that spits water when pressed is the bladder's death certificate, and the part replacement is priced by the pump's model, not by this page.</li>
<li><b>Then the non-return:</b> the little valve that keeps the suction line full between runs - if the pump has to re-prime every start, air in the line explains both the dry-tapping start and the house's upstairs losing pressure first when several taps run; the valve lives where the pipe meets the tank or bore line, and its replacement is the plumber's thirty-minute job the desk will not dress up as a tap-wrench errand for the wrong-handed.</li></ol>
<h2>The boundary, printed straight</h2>
<p>Inside the pump head is a professional's room: the pressure switch's contacts, the capacitor, the impeller wear that shows as a pump that runs long and builds pressure slow - all are the electrician-or-pump-service conversation, and the 'adjust the switch screw' video is how houses end with pumps that never cut off and a burst line. The same paragraph covers the borehole itself: if the draw-down test says the source is thinning (pump runs to pressure, then the flow weakens as the well recovers slowly), the fix belongs to the pump-size conversation with the well's driller - cycling a dry bore is how a household pays twice.</p>
<h2>The quiet prevention tier</h2>
<p>The vessel keeps its air when the system is not asked to run bone-dry (the tank's minimum-level switch the overflow guide already preaches protects pump and bladder together); the taps and flushes get fixed when they drip - each one is a start signal over the cushion's lifetime; and the loose pipes get strapped (a pipe clip screwed home tight stops the banging that loosens the joint costing the wall). The desk files short-cycling as the system's polite complaint: the bill arrives at the motor only if the polite version is ignored.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Tap off, watch the cycling, knock the tank, gauge the air - the four-step evening that names the fault in the two cheap parts; and the fifth sentence that stays true whatever the fix: the head itself stays closed, because the pump that is repaired wrong is the water that stops arriving quietly, six weeks later, with a bigger invoice.</p>"""),

 ("sofa-leather-vinyl-care",
  "MAINTAIN",
  "The sofa's cracks start in the harmattan: the two-season care that keeps vinyl supple and leather honest",
  "What sun actually does to a covering, the clean-and-feed rhythm in the right season, and the three products the finish will resent.",
  """<p>Upholstery fails on a calendar, not an accident: the dry weeks pull the plasticisers out of vinyl and the moisture out of real leather, the afternoon sun's window-light bakes both on the side that faces it, and the first visible result - the pale sheen, the fine crazing at the seat's crease - is already the mid-stage of a process the household can only slow, never reverse. What the two seasons allow is a routine that takes an afternoon at each turn and decides how the fifth year looks.</p>
<h2>Know which covering the sofa actually is</h2>
<p>The test the sales floor does not offer: lift a seat cushion and look at the underside and the back panel - real leather's reverse is a fibrous suede, vinyl's is a cloth or knit grid; 'PU' and the coated-corrected classes sit between, and their care is the vinyl chapter's, because the finish on top is the thing being fed. The distinction earns its keep at the product step: leather's conditioners are oils and waxes that a vinyl cannot drink, and the vinyl's silicone dressings would leave leather greasy and its stitching damp. The label or the seller's own specification settles it in writing when the underside test is ambiguous.</p>
<h2>The rhythm, by season, with the three product bans</h2>
<ol><li><b>Harmattan's opening week - clean first:</b> the dust that rides the season is abrasive, and every sit grinds it into the finish like sandpaper on a lens; the weekly dry wipe (soft cloth, along the creases, the seams where the grit lines up like a grout) is the habit that prevents the crazing's first scratch. Real leather then gets its gentle soap-only clean - the damp cloth with the pH-neutral leather soap the maker's own care leaflet names, never the kitchen degreaser, never detergent foam that strips the finish's oils in one enthusiastic afternoon.</li>
<li><b>Harmattan's middle - feed:</b> leather takes a thin coat of its conditioner (the cream the maker sells or names - the desk's general sentence, since the product shelf changes and the finish's chemistry does not); vinyl gets its water-based protectant if the maker offers one, and silicone spray - the ban the next paragraph prints - is the shortcut that costs the next owner.</li>
<li><b>The rains - fight the other enemy:</b> damp is vinyl's mould-bed and leather's stain-maker; the room's airflow habit from the wardrobe and mattress pages applies to the seat's underside, and a sofa that has met a flooded floor gets the desk's clean-and-dry sequence from the flooded-room guide, not this page's polish.</li></ol>
<p>The three products the finish resents, in the order of their damage: <b>silicone dressings</b> (the instant shine that migrates the plasticiser it borrows - vinyl gleams for a week and cracks faster for years, and any future repair or re-cover finds the surface refuses adhesive); <b>the car-dashboard shine wipes</b> whose solvents lift printed vinyl's ink layer in patches that start under the magazine and end under the lamp; and <b>the sponge-and-soap of the general cleaner's day</b> - soaking the seam is how leather's backing swells and vinyl's cloth grid rusts through its printed face, the two slowest deaths upholstery has.</p>
<h2>Position is half the routine</h2>
<p>The afternoon sun is the single biggest aging variable the household controls: the sofa moved a metre from the window's beam, the blind's rule on the west side through the hottest hours, the throw across the seat that catches the light daily - each one buys years at zero maintenance cost, and the desk files the faded-backrest-and-bright-seat-front contrast as the evidence no conditioner can polish away. The cushion rotation week-about (the reversible ones flipped end for end as well) spreads the sun and the crease, and the crease is where the crazing starts, so the five-minute habit is the anti-crack treatment the marketing never mentions.</p>
<h2>What no care routine reaches</h2>
<p>Split vinyl that has gone through to its cloth, the leather's dry rot at the seams where the backing has powdered, the foam that has collapsed and now flexes the finish past its stretch - all are re-cover conversations, and the honest re-cover economics belong to the workshop's quote, not to this page's shelf. The routine's promise, stated without flattery: on a sound sofa it doubles the life of the face and keeps it looking fed at year five; on a dead one it is a kind way to postpone the truth.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Wipe the dust weekly through the dry season, feed once at its middle, fight the damp at the rains, and keep the window off the face - four habits, two seasons, one afternoon's work each. Upholstery is the only furniture in the house that gets sat on, leaned on and sunned daily; it keeps the receipts in its creases, and the creases are the page's entire argument.</p>"""),

 ("gate-intercom-not-working",
  "FIX",
  "The gate buzz that buzzes and nothing else: the door-phone, traced from the button to the handset",
  "Two wires, three failure points and the corrosion the rains do: the sequence that answers 'who is at the gate' before the phone call to the technician.",
  """<p>The intercom is the most neglected circuit in the compound and the easiest one in the house to understand: a button, two wires, a handset - and when the system stops, the answer is one of three places, in order of how often they are the culprit: the button's contacts, the wire's joints, the handset's own speakers and hook-switch. A torch, a screwdriver and twenty honest minutes reach all three, and the call to the line technician that follows the check is a shorter, cheaper conversation.</p>
<h2>The system in one paragraph, then the checks in order</h2>
<p>Old-style wired door phones are simple by design: pressing the gate button rings the house unit and completes the talk circuit; the release (the gate opener) is the same system's third wire when it has one. The desk's starting move is the oldest telecom habit - find the signal where it should be: press the gate button while someone watches the house unit; if it rings, the problem is downstream of the bell (talk audio, the opener); if it never rings, the problem is upstream - the button, its wiring, or the unit's own answer. That one sentence halves the whole job before a single screw turns.</p>
<h2>The three culprits, with what each looks like when opened</h2>
<ul><li><b>The gate button:</b> outdoors, every day, on a metal post that sweats condensation - the contacts inside go green, the membrane tears on the third finger, the waterproof gasket's job fails when its cover fell off in 2019 and nobody filed its absence. The check is the screwdriver and a dry cloth, the test is the old short-the-terminals trick (bridge the two wires with a bit of spare core and press nothing - if the house unit rings with the button bypassed, the button is guilty and a replacement is the afternoon's purchase).</li>
<li><b>The run between:</b> two conductors buried in the compound's walls and along its fence are the wire that the rains interrogate - the terminal that wicks damp along the copper and turns it to green powder, the splice where the last tradesman joined a repair with two twists and tape, the stretch the gate's own swing has slowly pulled tight until one core parted at its crimp. The visual follows the cable with hands, squeezing the runs where the skin feels gritty (corrosion's own texture), and the electrical answer - continuity end to end at both ends with the wire off - belongs to the household member who owns the meter, or the hour is wasted on hope.</li>
<li><b>The house unit:</b> the ringer works while nobody can hear is the handset's speaker clogged with the decade (the grille, cleaned with a dry brush - not a pin, not water); the buzz with no voice is the microphone's foam or the hook-switch that never 'hangs up' electrically; the unit that is stone-dead while the wire carries its signal is the unit - and this desk adds the note the manual's footnote admits: many wired systems feed the call through the house's own landline or a powered adapter, and the power adapter's failure masquerades as the whole system's. The plug-in brick on the unit's wire is checked by feel (warm = doing something) and by the meter, before any other blame.</li></ul>
<h2>The opener's separate story</h2>
<p>If the buzz answers but the gate does not unlock: the release is its own circuit - the little electric strike or the solenoid on the motor's board that the talk wire commands for the two seconds of the click. The click with a stuck latch is mechanical (the bolt's spring, the gate's sag that pushes the strike out of alignment - the door-swell page's 'squares move' logic at compound scale); silence where a click should be is the opener's wire and its power, the same two-tests-above sequence applied one circuit over. A motor that does nothing on its remote but obeys the wall switch is the remote's story, and that one belongs to its own manual - battery, re-pair, dealer.</p>
<h2>The boundaries and the upgrade that ends the argument</h2>
<p>What the household does not touch: the mains side of any adapter it cannot unplug first, the motor's internals past its cover, and any wiring decision that means opening a bonded or live panel - the electrician's line runs here exactly as the socket pages print it. And the honest modern option, because the check above will sometimes end at 'the wire has copper rot through its whole run and re-pulling it is the compound's afternoon': the wireless intercom class - gate unit powered by its own battery, talk and release over radio, no trench, no green terminals - which the desk files as the cheap repair for the compound whose wired system is a demolition project, with the one caveat its listing states plainly: a battery is now part of the gate's maintenance calendar, which is exactly what the wired system's freedom from was worth.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Ring test first, then the button's contacts, then the run's hands-and-meter check, then the handset and its little power brick - the order that finds ninety percent of gate failures with a screwdriver, and the twenty minutes that turn the technician's visit from an investigation into a repair.</p>"""),

 ("standby-power-real-numbers-your-meter",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The decoder hums and the bill notices: standby power, measured on your own meter, not quoted from a chart",
  "What the idle boxes genuinely drink, the meter test that ends all forum arguing, and the switching habits that pay for themselves the first season.",
  """<p>Standby power is the bill's smallest villain and the most exaggerated one - the threads promise a haunting and the electricians shrug, because the honest answer is that a house's idle load is small per brick, sneaky in sum, and perfectly measurable by the household in ten minutes with the meter it already owns. This page skips the borrowed figures and does the arithmetic on your own house: which habits the measurement actually justifies, and which are folklore worth retiring.</p>
<h2>What the idle boxes really do</h2>
<p>A decoder in standby is a computer waiting to be woken - it keeps its tuner, its network and its memory warm, and that floor of continuous draw is the standby class the meter will show you. The television's fast-boot models never truly sleep either; the console and the stereo with the glowing dot are the same waiting logic. What idle draws less than folklore says: modern chargers with nothing on the other end (the transformer sips where the old iron ones burned), the led bulbs' negligible off-time, the router that never sleeps because the household forbids it. What still costs like the folklore: the old fridge in the store room with the door's failing seal (a compressor that cycles in the heat is the meter's loudest hum, and the room's ambient is its throttle - the coils and seal paragraphs of the fridge guide apply to the second fridge exactly as to the first), and anything with a heating element on a thermostat - the water heater's keep-warm habit is the standby load that dwarfs every little glowing dot in the house combined, and this desk's hot-water page states its switch-off case in full.</p>
<h2>The meter test, because your figures beat every chart</h2>
<ol><li><b>The quiet ten minutes:</b> at night, everything the household can safely off at the wall - decoders, televisions, consoles, the kitchen's little boxes - router and fridge staying on. Read the meter's pulse or the daily-usage screen's rate before and after; the difference is the house's true idle floor, in your tariff's own units.</li>
<li><b>The one-box test:</b> to see what a single device's standby costs, that device alone, off at its wall switch, same before-after reading over an hour - the number the forum argues about becomes the number in your notebook, and the multiplication (per day, times the tariff, times the year) decides whether a habit or a product is worth it.</li>
<li><b>The annualisation rule that stops the guilt: </b>small loads are per-year numbers; the decoder's idle that costs a month's phone credit over twelve months is real but not the bill's driver - the heater and the second fridge are the drivers, and the meter's daily figures across a fortnight show which category a complaint belongs to before any switch gets bought.</li></ol>
<h2>The switching habits that pay</h2>
<ul><li><b>The heater's own switch off at the wall when the house sleeps,</b> the tank's insulation's job instead of its element's - the hot-water page's standing line, repeated here as the house's single largest idle decision.</li>
<li><b>The strip with switches, not the strip that is a plug:</b> one night-tap kills the whole entertainment cluster's waiting, and the strip's own cost repays the season the meter shows - the desk's honest unit: strips that never get switched are decoration.</li>
<li><b>The decoder's standby versus its full off,</b> where the remote and the scheduler's updates matter, is a feature trade, not a savings scandal - measure it once and stop arguing about it; the old class of boxes that draw their running-watts idle (the brick that is warm in the hand while 'off') is the one the strip saves properly.</li>
<li><b>The second fridge's winter load:</b> the store-room box that runs a compressor alone in an empty building is switched by its contents' honesty; the drinks habit belongs to the evening and the kitchen's unit, and the meter's quiet-ten-minutes test on that room's circuit ends the decade of 'but it's small'.</li></ul>
<h2>The verdict, as a rule</h2>
<p>Measure your own idle floor before buying any of the internet's remedies: the heater's thermostat and the sweating second fridge are the standby bills in disguise; the little glowing dots are the meter's footnote, real, small and now quantified. The habit that captures most of the money costs one wrist per night - the strip switch at the television's cluster - and the rest of the folklore the test retires is folklore the house was already allowed to stop carrying.</p>"""),

 ("tiles-hollow-sounding-lifting",
  "UNDERSTAND",
  "The tile that sounds like a drum when you knock it: what hollow means, and when it means lifting",
  "Bed failure read from the knuckle test, the grout's real job (not waterproofing), and the map of hairline signs that say call before the tile chooses the foot.",
  """<p>A floor tile is a thin stone plate glued to a thick one, and the entire health of the floor is the glue's - so the hollow knock is not a mystery but a measurement: somewhere under that square, the bed has let go. Some hollow is a cosmetic pocket in the adhesive's comb-line; some is the first millimetre of a lift that will finish as a cracked plate and a loose edge at the doorway. The knuckle test, the map, and the three signs that turn a quirk into a call are this page in order.</p>
<h2>The bed, in the words the tiler's trade already uses</h2>
<p>Tiles hold because the adhesive wets both faces - the slab and the back - with a continuous bed. The failures are named and mechanical: too little coverage (the tiler's 'spot-buttering' - dabs at the corners and the middle, a drum-sound pocket in the rest that starts hollow and stays that way), the bed that dried before the tile landed (the open time the bag's own instructions warn about, ignored in the afternoon heat), the slab that was dusty or glossy when the bed went on (the primer's job, the same page's warning), and the movement under the floor - a slab's new crack, a wet area's flex - that the rigid bed answers by parting from one face or the other. Hollow, then, is evidence; the question the rest of this page answers is how much it is evidence of.</p>
<h2>The knuckle survey, and the three grades of answer</h2>
<ol><li><b>Walk the floor with a knuckle and an ear:</b> the tap along a line of tiles maps where the drum sound starts and ends; the edge lines of a room and the thresholds are the expansion joints' territory, where a degree of ring can be design rather than failure - the desk's note: perimeters ring, centres should not.</li>
<li><b>Hollow with no movement, hairline at most:</b> the pocket version - the tile is loose in its bed but its neighbours hold it; the honest advice is 'monitor and mind the grout', and the tile's own eventual fate is decided by which of the signs below appear.</li>
<li><b>Hollow plus lippage, a lifting edge or the crack that travels:</b> the bed is failing across an area, and the tile is now a lid over a void with feet as the hammer - the area gets the professional lift-and-relay (the slab assessed, the bed redone to wet-both-faces, the tile either re-used with fresh adhesive or replaced), which is the trade's day's work, not the DIY adhesive-tube story the product videos oversell: a re-stuck tile over a dirty pocket is the drum sound with a newer lid.</li></ol>
<h2>Grout's real job, stated once, loudly</h2>
<p>The grout line is a joint's filler and the floor's cosmetic seam - it is not a waterproofing layer, and the bathroom's leak that travels under the tiles and out at the doorway is the failure grout never promised to prevent (the bathroom-paint and leak pages tell that story from the wall side). What grout failure does mean: a crumbled line lets water into the bed's edge repeatedly, and a wet bed under a hot floor cycles - the wet-dry pump that loosens good tiles. So the survey's cousin: press a fingernail into the grout's line at the shower's edge and the threshold; sound grout is gritty and firm, dead grout powders - re-grouting the wet zone's perimeter is the maintenance that keeps the bed's edge dry, and it is the only part of this page that is genuinely the household's own afternoon.</p>
<h2>The signs that map the diagnosis</h2>
<ul><li><b>The new crack in one tile that meets the crack in the next in a straight line</b> - the floor is following a slab movement; the wall-cracks page's tape-test logic applies with furniture, and this is the structural conversation, not the tiler's alone.</li>
<li><b>The lifted edge at the doorway or the balcony line</b> - the perimeter's expansion gap was packed or never left, and the floor is now pushing against the house; the pop that relieves the pressure is one footstep away.</li>
<li><b>The hollow that spreads week to week</b> on the same map - the bed is unzipping; the sooner the area is lifted the more of the tiles come up re-usable, because the prying that waits until cracking is the tile's funeral.</li>
<li><b>The wet patch with no leak above it,</b> near the shower or the yard line - read it as the bed's own channel; the waterproofing beneath the tile (the membrane the wet-area rules require) is the layer under trial, and that investigation is the professional's, with the plumbing pages beside it.</li></ul>
<h2>The prevention sentence the trade will repeat</h2>
<p>A sound floor is the tiler's choices on day one - primed slab, open time respected, bed combed and back-buttered where the tile's size demands it, expansion gaps at the perimeters, the wet zones' membrane intact - which is why this desk prints the selection note as the only real maintenance advice: the relaid floor's quality is the same tradesman's second chance, so the re-quote asks the same questions the first job skipped, and the bag's own instructions on the adhesive's class for the floor's use (the internal, the wet, the flexible) is the page no tiler's habit should overrule.</p>
<h2>The desk's standing rule</h2>
<p>Knock the map before you fear the map: the ring with no movement is a pocket to watch, the ring with the lift is a relay to price, and the grout at the wet zones is the free re-do that slows both - the floor's drum sound is its bed reporting; this page exists so the household knows which of the three sentences the knock is saying.</p>"""),

]
